{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1787175154217087001,"compiled_state":{"alias":"icicled-unstable-urial","assignee":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","attestations":{"completion-verifier":{"algorithm":"sshsig","author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","envelope":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.opcert.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","head_sha":"42076d367e959c24ab8673598d1d69bfef9be803","key_id":null,"kind":"completion-verifier","manifest":["completion-verifier: PASS","ticket: 9305-b42c-3262-4c58","model: bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","runner: pydantic_ai","rebar-version: 0.10.1 (f374062ac9-dirty)","material: 56ec4329039e600f","verified-at-sha:42076d367e959c24ab8673598d1d69bfef9be803"],"material_fingerprint":"56ec4329039e600f","merged_log_commit":"42076d367e959c24ab8673598d1d69bfef9be803","principal":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","signature":null,"signed_at":1785876500239552000},"plan-review":{"algorithm":"sshsig","author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","envelope":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.opcert.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","head_sha":"f70e5d21013f6d81139ab3c4a146d264e3f4eb4d","key_id":null,"kind":"plan-review","manifest":["plan-review: PASS","ticket: 9305-b42c-3262-4c58","material: 56ec4329039e600f","model: bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","runner: pydantic_ai","blocking: 0","advisory: 3","review-phase: planning","rebar-version: 0.10.1 (f70e5d2101-dirty)","regver: 98022a04a68b0fef","verified-at-sha:f70e5d21013f6d81139ab3c4a146d264e3f4eb4d","dep 0607dd576289f64519251d7cbb2253e27284aaa853e0d17f7ddbd2333d46133a src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py","dep 7558462ede908237d606d8510c4106a69dcacc7a03bb3aaa43df4c9e0ca53c8a src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py","dep 95f1aa199593685eb9c56bd02db2f71f4f9ff4ca9acf09d8bfe8d65986bbdf81 src/rebar/_store/lock.py","dep 1238ff01bd5e7c9ad3c162babe251144a84e94684e477080b1be5223945c0862 src/rebar/metrics/blame.py","dep c6777f19010c337c2086cbb2662aebe405ced9b352be3e2588c9e30e035e818c src/rebar/metrics/git_metrics.py","dep absent tests/interfaces/store/test_git_contention_9305.py"],"material_fingerprint":"56ec4329039e600f","merged_log_commit":"f70e5d21013f6d81139ab3c4a146d264e3f4eb4d","principal":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","signature":null,"signed_at":1785864225289841000}},"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":32,"unsigned":3},"authorship_ledger":[],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"close_class":"preexisting","comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"--body","timestamp":1785542841539037001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"--body","timestamp":1785543437303852001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"PRIOR RESEARCH — OSS practice for programmatic git (commissioned 2026-07-31 for bug 747f; recorded\nhere because 747f is closed and this is the live ticket for the class).\n\nNOTE: this ticket also carries junk comments whose body is the literal text \"--body\". Those are mine:\n`rebar comment` takes a POSITIONAL body (`rebar comment <id> <body>`), not a `--body` flag, so the\ntext was discarded. This comment is the re-write. Ignore the \"--body\" entries.\n\n== 1. Git batches missing-blob fetches ONLY via unpack-trees ==\nDocumentation/technical/partial-clone: \"checkout (and any other command using unpack-trees) has been\ntaught to bulk pre-fetch all required missing blobs in a single batch.\" Confirmed in source:\nunpack-trees.c calls prefetch_cache_entries() inside check_updates(), gated on\nrepo_has_promisor_remote(); check_updates() runs ONLY when the working tree is updated.\nbuiltin/checkout-index.c contains NO prefetch at all. So `read-tree <sha>` (no -u) + `checkout-index\n--all` is exactly the path git never taught to batch. That was 747f's real mechanism, NOT the filter alone.\n  https://git-scm.com/docs/partial-clone\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git/git/master/unpack-trees.c\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git/git/master/builtin/checkout-index.c\n\n== 2. Ecosystem rule: FILTER IFF YOU WILL NOT MATERIALIZE ==\nactions/checkout is the reference implementation (src/git-source-provider.ts): blob:none is applied\nONLY on explicit request, or when sparseCheckout means most blobs are never materialized. The default\nfull checkout is never filtered. No mature tool was found that filters a ref it will check out in\nfull and relies on lazy fetch.\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/actions/checkout/main/src/git-source-provider.ts\n\n== 3. Batched refill options, strongest first ==\n- Do not filter the ref you materialize (one RPC, server-side delta compression). Oldest, fully supported.\n- Route through unpack-trees (read-tree -u) so git batches for you. SOURCE-READING, NOT cited\n  practice — reproduce before relying on it.\n- git backfill (>= 2.49, EXPERIMENTAL): https://git-scm.com/docs/git-backfill\n- `git rev-list --objects --missing=print` -> batched fetch. The idiom git's own design doc names.\n- `git fetch --refetch` reapplies a changed filter but refetches EVERYTHING — wrong granularity.\n- `git cat-file --batch` as promisor prefetch: NO evidence it batches. Do not build on it.\n\n== 4. Do not latch promisor state onto a repo the tool does not own ==\nEvery mature tool examined does partial/shallow work in storage IT owns: cargo (~/.cargo/git/db, and\nit uses shallow not blobless), gclient/depot_tools (dedicated cache_dir), actions/checkout (throwaway\nCI workspace), Scalar (creates the blobless clone itself). EVIDENCE CAVEAT: this is a UNIFORM ABSENCE\nof counterexamples, not a written prohibition — a weaker evidence class, reported as such. Known\nfailure mode: once remote.origin.promisor + partialclonefilter latch, changing the remote breaks\npushes (desktop/desktop#16284). Established mitigations: separate clone/cache dir,\nGIT_DIR/GIT_WORK_TREE/GIT_INDEX_FILE isolation, --no-write-fetch-head.\n\n== 5. TIMEOUTS ON PROGRAMMATIC GIT: NO SETTLED PRACTICE (flagged as genuinely unsettled) ==\nGit has no --timeout on fetch. actions/checkout — the most-executed git automation in the ecosystem —\nships NO timeout on any git subprocess and relies on the CI job timeout. Practice splits between an\nouter-process timeout (Python subprocess timeout=) and git-native transport bounds, which are more\nprecise and UNDERUSED:\n  http.lowSpeedLimit / http.lowSpeedTime — \"If the HTTP transfer speed ... is less than\n  http.lowSpeedLimit for longer than http.lowSpeedTime seconds, the transfer is aborted.\"\n  Overridable via GIT_HTTP_LOW_SPEED_LIMIT / GIT_HTTP_LOW_SPEED_TIME. Plus\n  GIT_SSH_COMMAND=\"ssh -o ConnectTimeout=...\" for SSH.\nThey COMPOSE: a subprocess timeout bounds total wall time; http.lowSpeed* kills a stalled connection\nin seconds rather than burning the whole budget.\n>>> DIRECTLY RELEVANT HERE AND TO 983f: rebar uses ONLY the outer-process form (_GIT_TIMEOUT) and\nsets NO git-native transport bound anywhere. Consider that gap as part of this ticket.\n\n== 6. GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH — version floor corrected, and it CANNOT be a guard ==\nDocumented/user-facing from commit e6d5479 (2024-02-27), shipping in git 2.45.0 — NOT 2.43 as bug\n747f's text claimed. Git silently ignores env vars it does not know, so on git < 2.45 it FAILS OPEN\nwith no signal — useless as a production guard on exactly the old-git environments where you would\nmost want one. Legitimate use is as a TEST ASSERTION (the docs position it alongside `git cat-file\n-e` as a probe). 747f shipped it that way.\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git/git/master/Documentation/git.adoc\n\n== Explicitly flagged as having NO settled ecosystem practice ==\n- Timeouts on programmatic git network calls (see 5).\n- Whether a tool may convert a repo it does not own into a partial clone (see 4 — uniform absence only).\n- git backfill semantics (documented as subject to change).\n- git cat-file --batch as a promisor prefetch mechanism (unverified).","timestamp":1785543505118372001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"OVERLAP WITH c2ba-98bf-ca2d-481a (rewrite of a lost \"--body\" comment).\n\nc2ba (filed from 1a47) independently proved that src/rebar/_store/event_append.py runs its lock-held\n`git add`/`git commit` with NO timeout= (unlike push.py). That is the SAME site this ticket lists,\nnow with a RUNTIME PROOF attached rather than a static sweep — treat c2ba's evidence as input here.\n\nc2ba's other half is BROADER than this ticket and NOT covered by it: review_bot/voter.py calls the\nsynchronous emit_code_review_artifact directly on the event loop. Measured: shutdown took 3.00s\nagainst a declared 0.2s bound, and a 0.1s heartbeat showed a single 2.01s gap. Consequence: the\ndrain's wait_for cannot fire, cancel() cannot be delivered, and `await task` is unbounded — so the\nworst-case store write is NOT COMPUTABLE, and the 150s stop_grace_period shipped under 1a47 is a\nlarge risk reduction rather than a proof.\n\nClosing c2ba means changing the production review path's CONCURRENCY MODEL — a design decision, not\na timeout value. The OSS research commissioned for this ticket should inform BOTH. Do not settle\nc2ba's concurrency question before those findings land.","timestamp":1785543545398814001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"OSS RESEARCH — LOCAL CONTENTION HANDLING IN MATURE PROJECTS (commissioned 2026-07-31)\n\n>>> CORRECTION BY THE COORDINATOR, READ FIRST <<<\nThe report's \"Grounding note\" claims commit 5ef138d26 (the 304e boot-id fix) is NOT reachable from\nmain and that lock.py still carries the pre-fix `_owner_stamp()`. THAT IS WRONG. The researcher\nmeasured against a STALE LOCAL CHECKOUT (HEAD 2a3abe6ab) instead of origin/main. Verified by me:\n`git merge-base --is-ancestor 5ef138d26 origin/main` => TRUE; origin/main's _store/lock.py contains\nthe v2 stamp (`rebar-lock v2 host=... ns=... pid=... start=...`, _STAMP_V2_PREFIX at :177).\nCONSEQUENCE: priority item #2 in section 5d (\"Land fix-304e-container-lock on main\") is ALREADY DONE\n— ignore it. Everything else in the report stands. (This is the same stale-worktree trap that bit me\nearlier tonight: always read `git show origin/main:<path>`, never the working tree.)\n\nEvery section separates CITED (a URL backs it) from EXTRAPOLATION (inference).\n\n== 1. LOCAL GIT CONTENTION (index.lock / HEAD.lock / packed-refs.lock) ==\n\n1a. What git gives you — CITED. Git HAS retry-with-backoff and deliberately does NOT use it for the\nindex. Generic path `lock_file_timeout()` in lockfile.c: quadratic backoff (multiplier += 2n+1, cap\nBACKOFF_MAX_MULTIPLIER 1000, base INITIAL_BACKOFF_MS 1) with EXPLICIT JITTER —\n`wait_ms = (750 + rand() % 500) * backoff_ms / 1000`, commented \"back off for between 0.75*backoff_ms\nand 1.25*backoff_ms\". Retries only on EEXIST. timeout_ms==0 => one attempt; -1 => forever.\nTHE INDEX TAKES THE ZERO-TIMEOUT PATH: repo_hold_locked_index() takes no timeout and calls\nrepo_hold_lock_file_for_update(), the wrapper passing timeout_ms=0 (repository.c L471-478,\nlockfile.h L223-230). So .git/index.lock = one attempt, no retry, NO CONFIG KNOB.\nThe three knobs that exist (Documentation/config/core.adoc L580-598): core.filesRefLockTimeout\n(default 100ms), core.packedRefsTimeout (default 1000ms), core.configLockTimeout (default 1000ms).\n0 = no retry, -1 = indefinite. No index equivalent.\nWhy refs got retry: Michael Haggerty's thread \"Retry acquiring reference locks for 100ms\" —\npack-refs and reflog expire lock refs WITHOUT changing their values, so retry has a real chance;\nGitHub saw ~10-50 such errors/day fleet-wide (~1 in a million updates), near-zero after retry.\nJeff King argued packed-refs needs a longer timeout since it is O(number of refs). The thread never\ndiscusses an index.lock equivalent.\nNew on git master (post-2025): core.lockfilePid writes a sibling foo~pid.lock containing \"pid <n>\",\nPURELY DIAGNOSTIC. On failed acquire, unable_to_lock_message() probes kill(pid,0) and emits an honest\nhedge: \"if no git process is running, the lock file may be stale (PIDs can be reused)\". Adds\ndiagnosis, not retry.\nEXTRAPOLATION: found no message explicitly REJECTING an index-lock timeout knob; the absence is\nevidenced by source/docs, not a stated decision. Plausible reason: index.lock is held across\narbitrary user work (conflicted merge, interactive add) where \"wait longer\" is not a meaningful bet,\nwhereas ref locks are held for microseconds by maintenance.\n\n1b. Do tools set the knobs? — CITED. YES, the largest one does. Gitaly forces both globally on every\ngit invocation, reasoning inline (internal/git/gitcmd/command_factory.go L752-763):\n  \"Git has a default timeout of 1 second to try and lock the file. In practice though we see that\n   this is not sufficient, and especially the DeleteRefs RPC is erroring out very frequently. We thus\n   increase the timeout to 10 seconds. While comparatively high, context cancellation would still\n   cause us to exit early in case the caller doesn't want to wait this long.\"\n  core.packedRefsTimeout = 10000; core.filesRefLockTimeout = 1000 (\"we have still observed lock\n  contention around them though, but mostly in cases where the host system was heavily loaded by a\n  storm of incoming RPCs\"). Driving issue: gitaly#5160.\nEXTRAPOLATION: outside Gitaly this appears rare — no other project found doing it. It is cheap\n(`git -c core.packedRefsTimeout=...`) and UNDER-USED.\n\n1c. The four mitigations — CITED.\n  (a) Retry with jittered backoff: git lockfile.c; JGit RETRY_SLEEP_MS = [0,100,200,400,800,1600]\n      ~3.1s for packed-refs (RefDirectory.java); lazygit 5x50ms then forcible unlink (commit 718e6d3).\n  (b) Serialize behind your own lock: JGit holds a FAIR in-process ReentrantLock\n      (inProcessPackedRefsLock = new ReentrantLock(true)) BEFORE touching the file lock; git-annex's\n      joeyh fixed a user's index.lock failures by \"only allow one git queue to be flushed at a time\".\n  (c) GIT_INDEX_FILE / worktree isolation: git-annex withIndexFile -> .git/annex/index\n      (Git/Index.hs, Annex/GitOverlay.hs); GIT ITSELF does it in `git stash`\n      (GIT_INDEX_FILE=$stash_index_path, builtin/stash.c) and git-filter-branch.sh L286-287.\n      GOTCHA git-annex documents: the path MUST BE ABSOLUTE — a relative one resolves against the\n      worktree top and is perturbed by GIT_WORK_TREE/core.worktree.\n  (d) Avoid the index (plumbing): git-lfs's hot path (clean/smudge/filter-process) touches no index —\n      a code search for index.lock across git-lfs/git-lfs returns ZERO hits; index use is confined to\n      `git update-index -q --refresh` in git/git.go. Gerrit runs bare through JGit\n      ObjectInserter/BatchRefUpdate, never a DirCache.\nBEST-ARGUED STATEMENT OF THE PATTERN — git-annex prepareModifyIndex (Annex/Branch.hs L387-412),\nwhich deletes any .git/annex/index.lock it finds, justified as: \"Since all modifications of the index\nare performed inside this module, and only when the journal is locked... no other process is\ncurrently modifying the index. So any index.lock file must be stale.\"\n=> THE LICENCE CONDITION for unlinking a git lock: OWN THE INDEX FILE EXCLUSIVELY **AND** HOLD YOUR\nOWN LOCK OVER IT. Absent that, blind unlink is unsafe. lazygit's unconditional os.Remove and GitLab\nRunner's delete-before-checkout are UI/CI shortcuts assuming single-writer.\nNOTABLE NEGATIVE RESULTS — CITED: dulwich does exactly one O_EXCL open and converts FileExistsError\nstraight into FileLocked — no loop, no timeout, no staleness check (dulwich/file.py L168-283).\nlibgit2's filebuf.c lock_file() returns GIT_ELOCKED immediately with no retry and implements NONE of\ngit's ref-lock timeout semantics (filebuf.c L44-58) — so LIBGIT2-BASED TOOLS ARE MORE LOCK-FRAGILE\nTHAN THE C GIT CLI EVEN ON REFS. JGit's DirCache.lock() mirrors C git and does not retry the index.\n\n== 2. TIMEOUTS ON PURELY LOCAL GIT SUBPROCESS CALLS — NO SETTLED ANSWER ==\nCITED FOR: Gitaly bounds EVERY git command with a Go context deadline, in the same breath as raising\nthe lock timeouts — \"context cancellation would still cause us to exit early in case the caller\ndoesn't want to wait this long\". GitLab layers per-request Gitaly timeouts above that (MR 224510).\nCITED AGAINST (the observed limit of the practice): where projects added git timeouts they scoped\nthem to NETWORK ops. Zuul's \"Add git timeout\" bounds remote clone/fetch at 300s and DELETES THE LOCAL\nREPO on timeout, explicitly because GitPython's clone_from()/fetch() lack hard timeouts; local\noperations untouched. GitPython's reported hangs (#547, #969) are clone/fetch. dulwich, libgit2,\ngit-lfs and git-annex bound NOTHING at the subprocess level.\nEXTRAPOLATION — the honest synthesis: bounding a purely local git call is UNUSUAL IN APPLICATION CODE\nAND STANDARD IN SERVER INFRASTRUCTURE. The dividing line is not local-vs-network, it is \"IS THERE A\nCALLER WITH A DEADLINE?\" Gitaly has one (an RPC); a CLI does not.\n  FOR: an unbounded local call can stall indefinitely on a stuck peer lock, a hung NFS/FUSE/network\n  mount, or a wedged filesystem — and rebar's calls run WHILE HOLDING THE STORE WRITE LOCK, so one\n  stall converts into a fleet-wide stall of every other agent. Failure without a bound is\n  indistinguishable from slowness.\n  AGAINST: a timeout can abort legitimately slow work (large gc, repack, cold-cache add on a huge\n  tree) and, worse, can abort it MID-WRITE — which for git means leaving the very .lock files you\n  were trying to avoid. Zuul's mitigation (delete and re-clone) is NOT available to rebar's tracker.\nRECONCILABLE POSITION: a WATCHDOG-GRADE bound (generous, 2-10x worst legitimate case) is defensible;\na LATENCY-GRADE bound is not.\n\n== 3. STALE-LOCK RECLAMATION: FOUR SCHEMES ==\nTHE DIRECT PRIOR ART — MERCURIAL — CITED. mercurial/lock.py already contains rebar's 304e fix:\n  def _getlockprefix():\n      \"\"\"Return a string which is used to differentiate pid namespaces\n      It's useful to detect \"dead\" processes and remove stale locks with confidence. Typically it's\n      just hostname. On modern linux, we include an extra Linux-specific pid namespace identifier.\"\"\"\n      result = encoding.strtolocal(socket.gethostname())\n      if pycompat.sysplatform.startswith(b'linux'):\n          try: result += b'/%x' % os.stat(b'/proc/self/ns/pid').st_ino\n          except (FileNotFoundError, PermissionError, NotADirectoryError): pass\n      return result\nFOUR DESIGN POINTS WORTH STEALING, same file:\n 1. THE LOCK IS A SYMLINK WHOSE TARGET IS THE STAMP — creation and content are ONE ATOMIC STEP:\n    \"lock is symlink on platforms that support it, file on others. symlink is used because create of\n    directory entry and contents are atomic even over nfs. old-style lock: symlink to pid.\n    new-style lock: symlink to hostname:pid\"\n 2. BREAKING A STALE LOCK IS ITSELF DONE UNDER A SECOND LOCK. _testlock(): \"if locker dead, break\n    lock. must do this with another lock held, or can race and break valid lock\" — takes <name>.break\n    with timeout=0, RE-READS THE STAMP, and only then unlinks.\n 3. FOREIGN PREFIX => NEVER BREAK (_lockshouldbebroken: if host != lock._host: return False).\n 4. BOUNDED WAIT WITH A WARNING STAGE: lock() sleeps 1s per attempt down from ui.timeout, DEFAULT\n    600s, with ui.timeout.warn (default 0) driving \"waiting for lock on ... held by ...\" and a\n    \"got lock after %d seconds\" follow-up (configitems.toml).\nWHAT MERCURIAL DOES NOT HAVE: no boot id, no process start-time qualification, NO AGE CEILING.\nTHE CONTAINER FAILURE MODE, BOTH DIRECTIONS — CITED:\n - rebar's direction (same host misread as foreign => orphan permanent): hostname is the container id\n   and changes on recreate. rebar 5ef138d26 documents field data: 4f2e3e889406 -> 3790c7a6034a while\n   boot id identical.\n - THE INVERSE, MORE DANGEROUS DIRECTION (foreign misread as stale => a LIVE lock broken):\n   restic#4922 — running restic inside Flatpak, \"when considering whether a lock from a restic\n   process outside the container is stale or not, it (correctly) sees the same hostname but then\n   can't see the pid and always decides the lock is stale.\" Same hostname, different PID namespace.\n   THIS IS THE CASE rebar's `ns=` FIELD GUARDS.\n - boot_id IS GENUINELY HOST-WIDE, NOT NAMESPACED — the request to containerize it was closed\n   WONTFIX, Lennart Poettering: \"PID namespaces have many users, beyond systems which try to boot-up\n   systems in them... Overmounting the boot id in userspace is very easy\" (RH bug 854604).\n   Consequences: (i) boot_id is the right host identity — CITED; (ii) trivially spoofable by an\n   overmount and absent on non-Linux — CITED for mechanism, EXTRAPOLATION for risk.\nCOMPARISON:\n - pid + start-time: sound on one host+namespace. Footguns: starttime is clock-tick granular\n   (/proc/pid/stat field 22); unavailable on macOS/Windows (degrades to bare pid); meaningless across\n   pid namespaces. Kernel devs call starttime \"useful to detect PID reuses in a somewhat reliable\n   way\" but tick-granular (LKML). filelock implements it: \"A PID that no longer exists is gone; a\n   live PID whose start token differs is a recycled PID\".\n - KERNEL flock/fcntl: SOUNDEST — auto-released on death, no reclamation logic to get wrong.\n   Footguns: advisory only; broken/absent on NFS; POSIX fcntl locks are LOST ON ANY close() of the\n   fd, hence OFD locks. cargo SKIPS LOCKING ENTIRELY ON NFS because \"the failure mode for flock on\n   NFS is BLOCKING FOREVER, even if the 'non-blocking' flag is passed\" (flock.rs). Bazel prefers OFD\n   locks — \"POSIX locks can be lost 'accidentally' due to any close() on the lock file, and are not\n   reliably preserved across execve()\" (blaze_util_posix.cc).\n - LEASE/TTL + HEARTBEAT: sound, and THE ONLY SCHEME THAT CANNOT WEDGE FOREVER. Footguns: needs clock\n   sync; a paused/swapped holder can have its live lock stolen. restic: staleLockTimeout = 30min,\n   refreshed every 5min, refreshabilityTimeout = staleLockTimeout - defaultRefreshInterval*3/2 \"to\n   compensate for a small time drift between clients\". proper-lockfile (yarn 1): mkdir strategy,\n   stale 10s, mtime heartbeat every stale/2, and it AVOIDS O_EXCL because \"O_EXCL is broken on NFS\n   file systems\". flufl.lock has explicit lifetime + refresh.\n - BOOT-ID QUALIFICATION: sound for HOST identity, strictly better than hostname. Linux-only;\n   spoofable by overmount; does NOT identify the namespace (needs the ns= companion).\nTHE HYBRID GIT ITSELF USES, THAT NOBODY NAMES: `git gc`'s gc.pid combines hostname + pid PLUS A\nGENEROUS TTL BACKSTOP, in lock_repo_for_gc() (builtin/gc.c L718-778):\n  /* 12 hour limit is very generous as gc should never take that long. On the other hand we don't\n     really need a strict limit here, running gc --auto one day late is not a big problem. --force\n     can be used in manual gc after the user verifies that no gc is running. */\n  time(NULL) - st.st_mtime <= 12 * 3600 &&\n  fscanf(fp, scan_fmt, &pid, locking_host) == 2 &&\n  /* be gentle to concurrent \"gc\" on remote hosts */\n  (strcmp(locking_host, my_host) || !kill(pid, 0) || errno == EPERM);\nREAD CAREFULLY: a FOREIGN HOST'S STAMP IS UNCONDITIONALLY RESPECTED (the conservative rule rebar\nshares) — BUT the 12-hour mtime ceiling means it can never wedge the repository FOREVER.\n*** THAT COMBINATION IS THE PIECE REBAR IS MISSING. ***\nYarn Berry states the asymmetry that makes liveness probes safe: \"It's not foolproof (there are false\npositive), but there are no false negative and that's all that matters in 99% of the cases\" — a false\n\"alive\" makes you wait (safe); a false \"dead\" makes you break a live lock (corruption).\nBazel's rule is the cleanest risk-elimination: it writes pid/owner/cwd/tty into the lock and comments\n\"The contents are printed for human consumption when another client fails to take the lock, but not\nparsed otherwise\" — ALL THE DIAGNOSTIC VALUE OF AN OWNER STAMP, ZERO PID-REUSE EXPOSURE, because the\nkernel lock is the only authority.\n\n== 4. BLOCKING POLICY UNDER CONTENTION, AND THE STARTUP CASE ==\nWHAT MATURE TOOLS DO — CITED:\n cargo package cache: try_lock then KERNEL-BLOCK FOREVER; prints \"Blocking waiting for file lock on\n   package cache\" once. No timeout.\n Bazel client: 500ms poll loop FOREVER; prints owner dump then \"Waiting for it to complete...\";\n   --noblock_for_lock => exit LOCK_HELD_NOBLOCK_FOR_LOCK. NO --lock_timeout EXISTS (0 code hits).\n Bazel server: commandLock.wait(500) loop forever; LockingMode.ERROR_OUT escape.\n Mercurial: 1s poll to ui.timeout = 600s, with a warn stage; timeout=0 escape.\n Python filelock: poll, timeout=-1 = forever; multi-file backends use JITTERED EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF\n   with secrets.randbelow; timeout=0 = one attempt.\n portalocker: BOUNDED — DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5s, DEFAULT_CHECK_INTERVAL = 0.25s, then raise.\n yarn 1: 200ms poll FOREVER, --mutex opt-in. yarn berry: 16.7ms poll, HARD 60s then throw.\n pnpm: NO STORE LOCK AT ALL. git (ref locks): jittered quadratic backoff to a config'd budget.\n git gc --auto: FAIL FAST, SILENTLY.\nPNPM'S ANSWER IS THE MOST RADICAL AND IT WORKS. zkochan, pnpm discussion #10702: \"It is safe to run\nmultiple installs concurrently, which modify the same store. All store related operations are atomic.\nThe store will never be left in a broken state. The store is not locked. When multiple installers use\nthe same store, they might redundantly download and extract the same package multiple times but it\nwill work.\" Contention DESIGNED OUT via atomic rename-based writes; the trade is redundant work.\n(Could not confirm the PR that removed the old directory locking — \"pnpm v6\" is UNVERIFIED.)\nNO SETTLED ANSWER: block-forever (cargo, Bazel, yarn 1) and bounded (portalocker, berry, Mercurial,\ngit) are both mainstream. Near-universal among block-forever: A VISIBLE, REPEATED WAITING MESSAGE\nNAMING THE HOLDER. Near-universal among bounded: JITTER.\nTHE STARTUP CASE — CITED. Git's own answer to \"a background/implicit path wants a lock\" is FAIL FAST,\nSILENTLY (builtin/gc.c L992-1001):\n  name = lock_repo_for_gc(force, &pid);\n  if (name) {\n          if (opts.auto_flag) { ret = 0; goto out; /* be quiet on --auto */ }\n          die(_(\"gc is already running on machine '%s' pid %\"PRIuMAX\" (use --force if not)\"), ...);\n  }\nAn EXPLICIT `git gc` dies loudly with the holder's host and pid. The IMPLICIT --auto path — the one on\nevery user's hot path, EXACTLY ANALOGOUS TO REBAR'S BOOT ENSURE SWEEP — returns success and does\nnothing. Housekeeping is opportunistic; it never blocks the thing the user asked for.\nBazel's --noblock_for_lock docs make the same point for automation: \"Developers might use this in\npresubmit checks to avoid long waits caused by another Bazel command in the same client.\"\nEXTRAPOLATION — the general rule: THE ACQUISITION BUDGET MUST BE STRICTLY SMALLER THAN THE DEADLINE OF\nWHOEVER IS WAITING ON YOU. rebar's incident was a 60s budget under a 30s health check — a\nstraightforward violation. COROLLARY THAT MATTERS MORE: fail-fast on a startup path must be LOUD IN\nTELEMETRY AND IDEMPOTENTLY RETRIED LATER, or you have converted a stall into silent skipped work.\n\n== 5. RECOMMENDATIONS FOR REBAR ==\n5a. DOES THE TWO-LEG flock+mkdir DESIGN MATCH, EXCEED, OR FALL SHORT?\nIt EXCEEDS mature practice on soundness of reclamation and FALLS SHORT on liveness guarantees.\nWHERE IT EXCEEDS (EXTRAPOLATION grounded in the cited comparisons):\n - The fcntl leg gives the kernel-mediated auto-release property cargo and Bazel rely on. The mkdir\n   leg gives the portable second window and NFS tolerance flock lacks. Neither leg waits on the other\n   across processes, so no deadlock. VERY FEW PROJECTS RUN BOTH.\n - The 304e stamp (rebar-lock v2 host=<boot-id|name> ns=<pidns-inode> pid=<pid> start=<starttime>) is\n   STRICTLY STRONGER THAN MERCURIAL'S hostname/nsinode:pid — it adds boot-id host identity and\n   start-time pid qualification, NEITHER OF WHICH MERCURIAL HAS.\n - The \"reclaim only under the fcntl proof\" rule for the cross-namespace case is GENUINELY NOVEL and,\n   as far as the researcher could tell, correct. NO PRIOR ART FOUND DOING THIS. Sounder than the\n   alternatives (mtime heuristic, or refusing forever).\n - rebar's colon-free v2 stamp, deliberately unparseable to an older reader, is BETTER\n   FORWARD-COMPATIBILITY HYGIENE than anything in the surveyed projects.\nWHERE IT FALLS SHORT:\n 1. NO AGE CEILING ANYWHERE. git gc has 12h, restic 30min, proper-lockfile 10s, flufl.lock a lifetime.\n    rebar has none. EVERY \"refuse without proof\" branch — absent stamp, malformed stamp, foreign host\n    identity — WEDGES THE STORE PERMANENTLY. That is the CLASS of the 304e incident; 304e fixed one\n    instance (the container-recreate identity) and LEFT THE CLASS OPEN.\n 2. NO JITTER. _acquire_fcntl sleeps a fixed 50ms, _acquire_mkdir a fixed 100ms, and\n    gitutil._with_index_lock_retry a linear 0.2 x attempt. Git jitters +-25% SPECIFICALLY to\n    de-synchronize competing processes. With many agents this is a THUNDERING HERD.\n 3. NO HOLDER DISCLOSURE TO THE WAITER. cargo, Bazel, Mercurial and yarn all tell the user who holds\n    the lock and that they are waiting. rebar waits SILENTLY for up to 60s then reports only a\n    duration. Bazel's \"print it, never parse it\" is free to adopt.\n5b. SHOULD LOCAL-ONLY GIT CALL SITES BE BOUNDED? YES, BUT AS A WATCHDOG, NOT A LATENCY BUDGET.\n 1. SET GIT'S THREE LOCK KNOBS ON EVERY INVOCATION — highest value per unit risk, and the only\n    fully cited-precedent move (Gitaly). Add\n    `-c core.filesRefLockTimeout=1000 -c core.packedRefsTimeout=10000 -c core.configLockTimeout=10000`\n    to the module-local _git helpers. Bounds HEAD.lock / packed-refs.lock / config.lock contention\n    INSIDE GIT where it belongs, and costs nothing when uncontended.\n 2. ADD A GENEROUS subprocess.run(..., timeout=...) WATCHDOG — 120s for ordinary plumbing, higher for\n    compact/fsck/gc-adjacent work. rebar's calls run WHILE HOLDING THE STORE WRITE LOCK, so an\n    unbounded stall on a hung mount converts one process's problem into every agent's — precisely the\n    coupling Gitaly's context deadlines prevent. Keep it well above any legitimate duration; the goal\n    is to distinguish \"wedged\" from \"slow\". EXTRAPOLATION: treat a timeout as a HARD FAULT (surface\n    it, release the lock) rather than retrying — an aborted git write is exactly what leaves .lock\n    residue.\n 3. DO NOT BOUND metrics/blame.py and metrics/git_metrics.py TIGHTLY. git blame and log walks over a\n    long-lived tickets branch are legitimately slow and hold NO store lock; a low bound would produce\n    spurious `unavailable` metrics. A high watchdog (e.g. 300s) is fine.\n 4. ADD JITTER to _acquire_fcntl (50ms), _acquire_mkdir (100ms) and gitutil._with_index_lock_retry\n    (0.2 x attempt). Copy git's shape: multiply each sleep by uniform [0.75, 1.25].\n 5. ADD AN AGE CEILING to _mkdir_lock_is_stale for the refuse-without-proof branches, following\n    git gc's 12-hour precedent. Choose far above any legitimate hold (the write lock is held for a\n    single event append — seconds), e.g. 1 hour. Preserves yaw-gravel-linen conservatism in the\n    normal case while REMOVING THE PERMANENT-WEDGE FAILURE CLASS ENTIRELY.\n    *** SINGLE HIGHEST-VALUE CHANGE, FULLY BACKED BY PRIOR ART. ***\n 6. GIVE THE BOOT PATH GIT'S --auto SEMANTICS. mcp_server.py:705 already passes timeout=5,\n    attempts=1 — good, consistent with gc --auto. But run_ensures logs LockTimeout at warning and\n    returns silently. Make the skip OBSERVABLE AND RE-ATTEMPTED: a metric/telemetry counter plus a\n    later opportunistic retry, so a permanently-contended store cannot silently never run its ensure\n    units.\n5c. FOOTGUNS THE TWO INCIDENTS DID NOT SURFACE:\n 1. THE mkdir -> OWNER-STAMP WRITE IS NOT ATOMIC, AND THE GAP WEDGES FOREVER. _acquire_mkdir does\n    os.mkdir(lock_dir) THEN opens and writes `owner`; the write is best-effort inside a bare\n    `except OSError: pass`. If the process dies in that window — or the stamp write fails —\n    _mkdir_lock_is_stale hits decision rule 1 (owner absent/unreadable => False) and the lock is\n    PERMANENTLY UNRECLAIMABLE, by exactly the 304e mechanism. The docstring acknowledges the window\n    and declines to act. MERCURIAL DESIGNED THIS OUT: the lock IS a symlink whose target is the stamp,\n    \"because create of directory entry and contents are atomic even over nfs\". An age ceiling (rec 5)\n    closes this too; a symlink lock closes it properly.\n 2. RECLAMATION DOES NOT RE-VERIFY UNDER THE BREAK LOCK. _acquire_mkdir calls _mkdir_lock_is_stale()\n    then _reclaim_mkdir_lock() as two separate steps. Mercurial's _testlock takes a SECOND lock\n    (<name>.break), RE-READS the stamp, and re-checks before unlinking, warning \"must do this with\n    another lock held, or can race and break valid lock.\" rebar's fcntl leg serializes Python\n    acquirers, covering the common case — but bash-era locks and dual_window=False callers exist, and\n    any writer taking the mkdir leg without the fcntl leg is outside that protection.\n    EXTRAPOLATION: low probability, HIGH SEVERITY (breaking a live lock => concurrent store writes).\n 3. LockHandle.release() UNLINKS UNCONDITIONALLY — removes `owner` and rmdirs without verifying the\n    stamp is still ours. If a peer ever did mis-reclaim (footgun 2), release would then delete that\n    peer's FRESH lock, cascading one misclassification into two. Mercurial guards release with\n    `if self._getpid() != self.pid: return`. Checking the stamp before unlink is a two-line hardening.\n 4. ON macOS THE v2 STAMP SILENTLY DEGRADES to hostname + bare pid. _read_boot_id,\n    _read_pid_namespace_id and _process_start_time all read /proc and all return None on Darwin, so\n    the stamp becomes host=name-<hostname> ns=- pid=<n> start=-. Decision rule 5 treats \"both unknown\"\n    as COMPARABLE, so it probes the pid WITH NO START-TIME QUALIFICATION. Given many concurrent local\n    agents and macOS as the operator's platform, THE PID-REUSE DEFENCE 304e ADDED IS NOT ACTIVE ON THE\n    PLATFORM WHERE CONCURRENCY IS HIGHEST. psutil.Process(pid).create_time() or `ps -o lstart= -p`\n    would restore it. CITED for the mechanism; EXTRAPOLATION for the exposure.\n 5. TWO MACS SHARING A FILESYSTEM WITH THE SAME DEFAULT HOSTNAME COLLIDE. With no boot id,\n    _host_identity() is name-<hostname>; macOS defaults are non-unique (MacBook-Pro.local). Two such\n    hosts on a shared volume would each judge the other's stamp \"same host\" and probe a meaningless\n    local pid — the restic#4922 hazard in its DANGEROUS direction (breaking a LIVE foreign lock).\n    EXTRAPOLATION, but the ingredients are cited.\n 6. run_ensures SWALLOWING LockTimeout IS A CORRECTNESS RISK, NOT JUST LATENCY. On a permanently\n    contended store the ensure sweep never runs and the only trace is a logger.warning. The migration\n    registry is idempotent, so a SKIPPED sweep is safe — but a NEVER-RUN sweep means the store\n    silently drifts from the schema every code path assumes. gc --auto gets away with this posture\n    because gc is pure housekeeping; MIGRATIONS ARE NOT. Needs a counter and an eventual forced sweep.\n 7. gitutil'S index.lock RECLAMATION IS MTIME-ONLY. _INDEX_LOCK_STALE_S = 300 with no pid or owner\n    check — the WEAKEST scheme in the survey (yarn 1 / proper-lockfile's model but WITHOUT\n    proper-lockfile's compensating heartbeat that keeps a live holder's mtime fresh). Git never\n    refreshes index.lock's mtime, so any git op legitimately holding the index >300s (a big repack, a\n    slow add on a cold FUSE mount) HAS ITS LOCK UNLINKED UNDERNEATH IT. git-annex's licence condition\n    is the standard rebar would need to meet, and it requires OWNING THE INDEX: see footgun 8.\n 8. REBAR DOES NOT USE GIT_INDEX_FILE ISOLATION, AND IT IS THE OBVIOUS FIT. The tracker worktree's\n    .git/index is shared with anything else touching that worktree. Both git-annex (.git/annex/index\n    via withIndexFile) and git itself (stash, filter-branch) point their own operations at a private\n    index file. rebar's event-append path is a small closed set of stages — a dedicated ABSOLUTE-PATH\n    GIT_INDEX_FILE would (a) eliminate index.lock contention with foreign writers outright, and\n    (b) RETROACTIVELY LICENSE the mtime reclamation in footgun 7, because rebar would then be the\n    provable sole owner. EXTRAPOLATION for rebar; CITED as the pattern.\n 9. _reclaim_mkdir_lock AND THE STAMP WRITE BOTH SWALLOW EVERY OSError. A read-only or full\n    filesystem, or a permissions problem, degrades silently into \"the lock can never be reclaimed\"\n    with no diagnostic. Compare _acquire_fcntl, which rebar recently and correctly hardened (commit\n    7b858c1a3, \"discriminate contention from real faults\") — the same discrimination has NOT been\n    applied to the mkdir leg.\n10. NON-ISSUE, VERIFIED — recorded so a future session does not re-litigate it: the fcntl fd is\n    created with os.open(lock_path, os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR) with no O_CLOEXEC, which would normally\n    risk leaking the lock into a long-lived child. PEP 446 makes Python-created fds non-inheritable by\n    default; confirmed os.get_inheritable(fd) is False on this interpreter. THE SAFETY IS REAL BUT\n    IMPLICIT — worth an inline comment, since explicit O_CLOEXEC costs nothing and git states the\n    property deliberately (\"the file descriptor returned by hold_lock_file_for_update() is marked\n    O_CLOEXEC\", lockfile.h).\n5d. PRIORITY ORDERING (coordinator note: item 2 below is ALREADY DONE — see the correction at top):\n 1. Age ceiling on all refuse-without-proof branches — git gc 12h / restic 30min / flufl lifetime.\n    Risk if skipped: PERMANENT STORE WEDGE — the 304e failure class, still open.\n 2. [ALREADY DONE — 5ef138d26 is on origin/main] Land fix-304e-container-lock on main.\n 3. Set the three core.*Timeout knobs on every git invocation — Gitaly. Risk: avoidable ref/config\n    lock failures under agent storms.\n 4. Jitter every retry sleep — git lockfile.c, filelock. Risk: thundering herd at high agent counts.\n 5. Watchdog timeout= on local git subprocesses — Gitaly context deadlines. Risk: one hung mount\n    stalls every agent.\n 6. Start-time qualification on macOS (psutil/ps fallback) — filelock, LKML. Risk: pid-reuse defence\n    absent on the highest-concurrency platform.\n 7. Atomic stamp (symlink lock) or re-verify under a break lock — Mercurial lock.py. Risk:\n    crash-in-window => permanent wedge; live-lock break.\n 8. Report the holder to the waiter (print, never parse) — Bazel, Mercurial, cargo. Risk: 60s of\n    silence is indistinguishable from a hang.\n 9. GIT_INDEX_FILE isolation for the event-append path — git-annex, git stash. Risk: index.lock\n    contention; unlicensed mtime reclamation.\n10. Make the run_ensures skip observable + eventually forced — gc --auto precedent, but migrations\n    are not housekeeping. Risk: silent schema drift on a busy store.\n\n== SOURCES ==\ngit: lockfile.c, lockfile.h, repository.c, builtin/gc.c, builtin/stash.c, git-filter-branch.sh,\nDocumentation/config/core.adoc; \"Retry acquiring reference locks for 100ms\" (public-inbox).\nMercurial: mercurial/lock.py, configitems.toml (foss.heptapod.net/mercurial/mercurial-devel).\nGitaly: internal/git/gitcmd/command_factory.go; gitaly#5160; docs.gitlab.com Configure Gitaly;\n  GitLab MR 224510.\ncargo: src/util/cache_lock.rs, src/util/flock.rs; cargo#9742; cargo#11566.\nBazel: src/main/cpp/blaze_util_posix.cc, blaze.cc, startup_options.cc,\n  BlazeCommandDispatcher.java; bazel.build/docs/user-manual.\nrestic: internal/repository/lock.go, lock_file.go; restic#4922; restic#2736.\ngit-annex: Annex/Branch.hs, Git/Index.hs, Annex/GitOverlay.hs, Utility/LockFile/PidLock.hs;\n  git-annex.branchable.com devblog \"day 336 pid locks\", forum \"parallel invocation\", pidlock race bug.\ngit-lfs: git/git.go. dulwich: dulwich/file.py. libgit2: src/util/filebuf.c.\nJGit: RefDirectory.java, LockFile.java, DirCache.java.\nlazygit commit 718e6d38b7e8611d3a8bea1248b35b897e672b11; GitButler#5390;\n  GitLab Runner MR 82; Azure DevOps index.lock doc.\nfilelock: src/filelock/_api.py, py-filelock.readthedocs.io concepts.\nportalocker: portalocker/utils.py, portalocker.readthedocs.io.\npnpm discussion #10702. yarn: src/cli/index.js; yarn berry FakeFS.ts; proper-lockfile (moxystudio).\nflufl.lock docs. RH bug 854604 (boot_id namespacing WONTFIX). LKML process starttime granularity.\nZuul commit ba1c8c0e310bbe4a874aaa81042b8f1d3e8ac078 (add git timeout). GitPython#547, #969.","timestamp":1785544817721361001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"OSS RESEARCH, PART 2 — GIT LOCK INTERNALS AND TOOLS THAT DRIVE GIT CONCURRENTLY (2026-07-31)\n\nCompanion to the previous comment. Deeper primary-source read. VERSION ANCHOR: git.git master\n@ a97fcc37c (2026-07-30); stable v2.55.0 (2026-06-29); prior v2.54.0 (2026-04-19). Two lock-relevant\nthings landed RECENTLY and are easy to miss: core.lockfilePid (in 2.54+) and core.configLockTimeout\n(merged to master 2026-07-30, NOT YET IN ANY RELEASE).\n\n== 1. GIT ITSELF ==\n\n1.1 THE LOCKFILE MODEL — and the key negative finding.\nlockfile.h L4-31: \"Mutual exclusion and atomic file updates... we create a lockfile <filename>.lock,\nwrite the new file contents into it, and then rename the lockfile to its final destination... with\nO_CREAT|O_EXCL... Automatic cruft removal. If the program exits after we lock a file but before the\nchanges have been committed, we want to make sure that we remove the lockfile. This is done by\nremembering the lockfiles we have created in a linked list and setting up an atexit(3) handler and a\nsignal handler that clean up the lockfiles.\" Also: \"lockfiles only block other writers. Readers do\nnot block, but they are guaranteed to see either the old contents of the file or the new contents.\"\n*** ON \"WHY DOES GIT DELIBERATELY NOT DO STALE-LOCK DETECTION\": THERE IS NO SUCH COMMENT, AND THAT IS\nITSELF THE FINDING. *** lockfile.h and tempfile.h contain ZERO occurrences of \"stale\". The lockfile\nAPI has no concept of lock ownership, age, or liveness. Staleness is not rejected by an argued design\nnote — it is simply OUTSIDE THE MODEL. A lock is a file that exists; cleanup is atexit+signal handlers\nin the owning process; anything surviving SIGKILL, OOM, power loss or container teardown is THE\nHUMAN'S PROBLEM. No mailing-list thread found arguing it either way.\n\n1.2 THE LOCK_* FLAGS (lockfile.h L155-178). There is NO \"LOCK_ON_ERROR\". The three are:\n  LOCK_DIE_ON_ERROR (1) — \"If a lock is already taken for the file, die() with an error message.\"\n  LOCK_NO_DEREF (2) — do not resolve symlinks; \".lock\" appended to the path argument itself.\n  LOCK_REPORT_ON_ERROR (4) — return -1 AND give the usual error message.\n  Default (flags 0) is SILENT -1.\n\n1.3 THE TIMEOUT MACHINERY. lockfile.h L180-190: \"If the file is currently locked, retry with\nquadratic backoff for at least timeout_ms milliseconds. If timeout_ms is 0, try exactly once; if\ntimeout_ms is -1, retry indefinitely.\"\nBackoff is quadratic WITH JITTER, capped per-sleep at 1s. From commit 044b6a9ef \"lockfile: allow file\nlocking to be retried with a timeout\" (Michael Haggerty, 2015-05-14): \"On each failed attempt, use\nselect() to wait for a backoff time that increases quadratically (capped at 1 second) and HAS A\nRANDOM COMPONENT TO PREVENT TWO PROCESSES FROM GETTING SYNCHRONIZED.\"\nCONFIGURED TIMEOUTS (Documentation/config/core.adoc L580-598):\n  core.filesRefLockTimeout   ms  default 100    0=no retry  -1=forever   shipped 2.15+\n  core.packedRefsTimeout     ms  default 1000   0=no retry  -1=forever   shipped 2.5+\n  core.configLockTimeout     ms  default 1000   0=no retry  -1=forever   MASTER ONLY, UNRELEASED\n  credentialStore.lockTimeoutMS ms default 1000                          shipped 2.30+\nWHY THE TWO REF NUMBERS DIFFER — the load-bearing rationale, and it also explains the index.\nFrom f4ab4f3ab \"lock_packed_refs(): allow retries when acquiring the packed-refs lock\" (2015):\n  \"This is not such a limitation for loose reference files. First, they don't take long to rewrite.\n   Second, MOST REFERENCE UPDATES HAVE A KNOWN 'OLD' VALUE, so if another process is updating a\n   reference at the same moment that we are trying to lock it, then probably the expected 'old' value\n   will no longer be valid, and the update will fail anyway. But these arguments do not hold for\n   packed-refs: * The packed-refs file can be large and take significant time to rewrite. * Many\n   references are stored in a single packed-refs file...\"\nFrom 4ff0f01cb \"refs: retry acquiring reference locks for 100ms\" (2017), which walked that back:\n  \"The philosophy of reference locking has been, 'if another process is changing a reference, then\n   whatever I'm trying to do to it will probably fail anyway because my old-SHA-1 value is probably\n   no longer current'. But this argument falls down if the other process has locked the reference to\n   do something that DOESN'T ACTUALLY CHANGE THE VALUE of the reference, such as pack-refs or reflog\n   expire.\"\nHaggerty's operational number (mailing list): before the patch, \"maybe 10-50 such errors every day\nacross our whole infrastructure\"; after, \"that number went basically to zero.\" Jeff King noted GitHub\nwas already running equivalent logic.\n\n1.4 index.lock: CONFIRMED — NO RETRY KNOB EXISTS, ANYWHERE.\nrepository.c L471-478 repo_hold_locked_index() -> repo_hold_lock_file_for_update(), defined in\nlockfile.h L236-241 as ..._timeout(..., 0) = TRY EXACTLY ONCE. All ~40 call sites (builtin/add.c:454,\nbuiltin/commit.c:534, builtin/checkout.c:630, apply.c:4966, add-interactive.c:645, ...) inherit that.\ngit grep over Documentation/, *.c, *.h for indexLockTimeout|index_lock_timeout|INDEX_LOCK_TIMEOUT\nreturns NOTHING. There is no core.indexLockTimeout and never has been. NO patch or RFC proposing one\nwas found; the 100ms ref-lock thread contains no index discussion at all.\nGIT'S CHOSEN ANSWER INSTEAD — commit 27344d6a6 \"git: add --no-optional-locks option\" (Jeff King,\n2017), the closest thing to an official position. It enumerates three options and REJECTS the\ncoordination one:\n  \"1. Have some kind of 'pseudo-lock' that allows other commands to tell status that they want the\n      lock. THIS IS LIKELY TO BE COMPLICATED AND ERROR-PRONE TO IMPLEMENT (and maybe even impossible\n      with just dotlocks to work from, as it requires some inter-process communication).\n   2. Avoid background runs of commands like 'git status'... This is awkward...\n   3. Ask 'status' not to lock or write the index. This is easy to implement.\"\nAnd why index contention differs from ref contention: \"taking the index lock may conflict with other\noperations in the repository. Especially ones that the user is doing themselves, which _aren't_\nopportunistic. In other words, 'git status' knows how to back off when somebody else is holding the\nlock, but other commands don't know that status would be happy to drop the lock if somebody else\nwanted it.\"\nSHIPPED MECHANISM: `git --no-optional-locks <cmd>` / GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 (Documentation/git.adoc):\n  \"If this Boolean environment variable is set to false, Git will complete any requested operation\n   without performing any optional sub-operations that require taking a lock. For example, this will\n   prevent 'git status' from refreshing the index as a side effect. THIS IS USEFUL FOR PROCESSES\n   RUNNING IN THE BACKGROUND WHICH DO NOT WANT TO CAUSE LOCK CONTENTION with other operations on the\n   repository. Defaults to 1.\"\nIt is an ENV VAR deliberately, so it propagates to subprocesses (Peff).\n*** READING: git's answer to \"many agents driving one repo\" is NOT \"wait for the index lock\" — it is\n\"DON'T TAKE IT.\" ***\n\n1.5 WHAT GIT TELLS THE USER, AND THE 2026 PID-FILE CHANGE.\nThrough v2.53 (unchanged since aed7480ca, 2016; wording origin e43a6fd3e, 2009 — \"Just saying that\nindex.lock exists doesn't tell the user _what_ to do\"): \"Unable to create '%s.lock': %s.\\n\\nAnother\ngit process seems to be running in this repository, e.g. an editor opened by 'git commit'. Please\nmake sure all processes are terminated then try again. If it still fails, a git process may have\ncrashed in this repository earlier: remove the file manually to continue.\"\nIn v2.54+ replaced by dbdcab6b8 \"lockfile: add PID file for debugging stale locks\" (Paulo Casaretto,\n2026-01-22). lockfile.c L254-299 picks one of three:\n  pid_status == 1  -> \"Lock may be held by process %ju; if no git process is running, the lock file\n                       may be stale (PIDs can be reused)\"\n  pid_status == -1 -> \"Lock was held by process %ju, which is no longer running; the lock file\n                       appears to be stale\"\n  else             -> \"Another git process seems to be running in this repository, or the lock file\n                       may be stale\"\nConfig core.lockfilePid (core.adoc L351-360), DEFAULT FALSE: \"Git will create a PID file alongside\nlock files... named by inserting ~pid before the .lock suffix... contains a single line in the format\n`pid <value>`.\" The `~` is deliberate (lockfile.h L132-137): \"The tilde is forbidden in refnames and\nallowed in Windows filenames, avoiding namespace collisions.\"\nTHREE THINGS TO TAKE FROM THIS:\n 1. Even in 2026, with dead-PID detection in hand, GIT STILL DOES NOT BREAK THE LOCK. It prints\n    \"appears to be stale\" and stops. The commit is titled \"for DEBUGGING stale locks.\"\n 2. Git annotates its own PID-liveness check with \"(PIDs can be reused)\" IN USER-FACING TEXT. It does\n    not trust pid liveness as proof.\n 3. The PID file records NO HOSTNAME — unlike gc.pid. On a shared/network filesystem the kill(pid,0)\n    check is therefore meaningless-to-actively-wrong. Off by default, so latent, but a real gap.\n\n1.6 gc.pid — GIT'S OWN HOSTNAME+PID STALENESS SCHEME (the one place it does break a lock).\nbuiltin/gc.c L718-781, predicate decoded: gc backs off only if ALL hold — pidfile younger than 12h;\nparses as \"<pid> <hostname>\"; AND (host differs — in which case we cannot check, so ASSUME ALIVE — OR\nkill(pid,0) says the local pid is alive, OR errno==EPERM).\n  /* 12 hour limit is very generous as gc should never take that long... running gc --auto one day\n     late is not a big problem. --force can be used in manual gc after the user verifies that no gc\n     is running. */\n  time(NULL) - st.st_mtime <= 12 * 3600 &&\n  fscanf(fp, scan_fmt, &pid, locking_host) == 2 &&\n  /* be gentle to concurrent \"gc\" on remote hosts */\n  (strcmp(locking_host, my_host) || !kill(pid, 0) || errno == EPERM);\nFailure message (gc.c:999): \"gc is already running on machine '%s' pid %ju (use --force if not)\".\nOrigin 64a99eb47 (2013): \"In the worst case, gc may be delayed 12 hours if a daemon reuses the pid\nstored in gc.pid.\" The design CONSCIOUSLY ACCEPTS A 12-HOUR FALSE-NEGATIVE WINDOW as the price of not\nracing.\nTHREE STRUCTURAL SAFEGUARDS WORTH NAMING: (a) a WALL-CLOCK CEILING (12h) as backstop for any\npid/hostname confusion; (b) an explicit HOSTNAME GUARD THAT FAILS CLOSED — a different hostname means\n\"assume running\", never \"assume stale\"; (c) errno==EPERM treated as alive (pid exists under another uid).\nCONTAINER CAVEAT, HONESTLY SCOPED: the mechanism has an obvious container failure mode (hostname is\nthe ephemeral container/pod id, so a repo on a mounted volume gc'd from a NEW container after a hard\nkill sees strcmp != 0 -> fails closed -> gc refuses FOR UP TO 12 HOURS). The inverse also holds if\nhostnames are reused. NO git-project bug report, mailing-list thread, or upstream acknowledgement of\neither case was found. Treat the mechanism analysis as sound (read from source) but the CONTAINER\nINCIDENCE as UNDOCUMENTED, not a cited known issue.\nRELATED: `git maintenance` does NOT use this scheme — gc.c L1784-1800 takes a plain\n$GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY/maintenance.lock with LOCK_NO_DEREF: no pid, no hostname, no timeout, no\nstaleness check. Same for schedule.lock at L3368. THE PID+HOSTNAME LOGIC IS `git gc` ONLY.\ngc.log: on --auto in daemonized mode stderr is captured into .git/gc.log.lock and committed to\n.git/gc.log iff non-empty (process_log_file(), gc.c L78-104). gc.logExpiry (config/gc.adoc L70-75):\n\"If the file gc.log exists, then 'git gc --auto' will print its content and exit with status zero\ninstead of running unless that file is more than 'gc.logExpiry' old. Default is 1.day.\"\n\n1.7 DOES GIT EVER AUTO-REMOVE STALE LOCK FILES? Only its own, only in-process. atexit(3) + signal\nhandlers (tempfile.c) roll back locks the CURRENT process holds, including on die(). Nothing scans\nfor or reaps foreign locks. SIGKILL, OOM, power loss or container SIGKILL leaves the .lock in place\nPERMANENTLY. The only exceptions in the whole tree are gc's guarded gc.pid takeover and the gc.log\nunlink.\n\n== 2. TOOLS THAT DRIVE GIT CONCURRENTLY ==\n\n2.1 git-lfs — NO index.lock HANDLING AT ALL (a negative example). @b9964490 (2026-07-28).\ngrep -rn \"index\\.lock\" over the tree: ZERO matches. grep -rni \"another git process\": zero.\nIt never opens .git/index; it shells out to `git update-index -q --refresh --stdin` (git/git.go:645\nUpdateIndexFromStdin), streamed through gitIndexer (commands/pull.go L185-234). Its ONLY concurrency\nguard is an in-process sync.Mutex serialising its own writes to that one subprocess's stdin. On\nfailure it logs and continues (pull.go:153) — that string is what users see when the child dies on\nindex.lock (#2937, #937, #2817). It has historically also CREATED the problem — #558 \"Failures during\na checkout can leave the git index locked\": killing update-index mid-stream orphans the lock. A PR\nsearch for index.lock in git-lfs returns 0 results; NO ONE HAS EVER ADDED HANDLING.\ngit-lfs does NOT use GIT_INDEX_FILE — the only occurrence is canonicalizeEnvironment()\n(commands/commands.go:391), which merely normalises an INHERITED value. So `git lfs pull`/`checkout`\ncontend for the real index. The locking/ package is unrelated (server-side LFS file locks over\nHTTP/SSH). No OS advisory locks anywhere: grep -rniE \"flock|LOCK_EX\" finds nothing. The one retry loop\nis Windows sharing-violation handling (robustRemoveRetryDelaysMs = [0,1,10,20,40]).\n\n2.2 git-annex — THE STRONGEST DESIGN IN THE ECOSYSTEM. (DataLad mirror, upstream/master @ e3a0558c.)\nFIRST, THE STRUCTURAL MOVE: git-annex NEVER CONTENDS FOR .git/index.lock. It commits to the git-annex\nbranch through a private index via GIT_INDEX_FILE — Git/Index.hs:17 (indexEnv = \"GIT_INDEX_FILE\"),\nAnnex/GitOverlay.hs L26-63 (withIndexFile -> .git/annex/index), used by Annex/Branch.hs:688.\nSECOND, WHEN IT DOES BREAK A LOCK IT DOES SO UNDER A PROVEN INVARIANT, NOT A TIMER —\nAnnex/Branch.hs L668-681 prepareModifyIndex: \"Since all modifications of the index are performed\ninside this module, and only when the journal is locked, the fact that the journal has to be locked\nwhen this is called ensures that no other process is currently modifying the index. So any index.lock\nfile must be stale, caused by git running when the system crashed, or the repository's disk was\nremoved, etc.\"\n=> THE GENERAL PATTERN: A HIGHER-LEVEL LOCK ESTABLISHES THE INVARIANT THAT MAKES BREAKING THE\nLOWER-LEVEL LOCK SAFE.\nTWO BACKENDS. Utility/LockFile.hs disclaims portability: \"Posix and Windows lock files are extremely\ndifferent. This module does *not* attempt to be a portability shim.\" Utility/LockFile/Posix.hs uses\nreal fcntl record locks with two details worth stealing: tryLock runs under uninterruptibleMask_ so\nasync exceptions cannot orphan a held fd; and checkSaneLock compares the fd's deviceID/fileID against\na fresh stat of the path — \"This check is useful if the lock file might get deleted by something\nelse\" (the classic fcntl unlink footgun).\nWHY PID LOCKS EXIST (man page): \"Normally, git-annex uses fine-grained lock files to allow multiple\nprocesses to run concurrently... That works great, unless you are using git-annex on a filesystem\nthat does not support POSIX fcntl locks. This is sometimes the case when using NFS or Lustre... And,\nsome networked filesystems are so inconsistent that one node can't reliably tell when the other node\nis holding a pid lock. CAVEAT EMPTOR.\"\nDefaults (Types/GitConfig.hs L255-257): annex.pidlock = False; annex.pidlocktimeout = 300 seconds.\n`git annex init` auto-enables by PROBING — taking a real exclusive lock in a temp dir and flipping the\nconfig on failure (Annex/Init.hs L458-464), with a 10s watchdog warning because the probe itself hangs\non sick NFS.\nTHE SIDE LOCK — THE GENUINELY NOVEL BIT. Joey Hess (devblog day 336): \"the fun part about pid locks is\nthat it can be pretty hard to tell if one is stale or not. Especially when using a networked\nfilesystem, because then the pid in question can be running on a different computer. Even if you do\nfigure out that a pid lock is stale, how do you then take over a stale pid lock, without racing with\nanother process that also wants to take it over?... PUT A MORE MODERN LOCK FILE SOMEPLACE ELSE (eg,\n/dev/shm) AND USE THAT LOCK FILE TO LOCK THE PID LOCK FILE. Then you can tell if a local pid lock file\nis stale quickly locally, and take it over safely.\"\nMechanism (Utility/LockFile/PidLock.hs):\n - Pid-lock CONTENT is PidLock { lockingPid, lockingHost } — hostname recorded exactly because the pid\n   may be on another machine.\n - Side lock is a real POSIX exclusive lock in /dev/shm (fallback /tmp), i.e. ON LOCAL STORAGE THE\n   NETWORK FS CANNOT LIE ABOUT; name = abs pid-lock path with / -> _, md5-prefixed if truncated.\n   Deliberately world-writable: \"Since /dev/shm and /tmp are sticky dirs, a user cannot delete another\n   user's lock file there, so could not delete a stale lock.\"\n - RELEASE ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING: dropSideLock UNLINKS BEFORE UNLOCKING — \"Delete the file first,\n   to ensure that any process that is trying to take the side lock will only succeed once the file is\n   deleted, and so will be able to immediately see that it's taken a stale lock\" — and dropLock drops\n   the side lock BEFORE the pid lock, \"at which point the pid lock will be considered stale.\"\n - TAKEOVER REQUIRES BOTH CONDITIONS: \"Since we have the sidelock, and are on the same host that the\n   pidlock was taken on, we know that the pidlock is stale, and can take it over.\" DIFFERENT HOSTNAME\n   => REFUSE, fall through to timeout. Residual limit stated plainly in the haddock: \"if the lock file\n   is on a networked file system, and was created on a different host than the current host\n   (determined by hostname), this can't be done and stale locks may persist.\"\n - Acquisition is link()-based per open(2)'s advice, verifying linkCount == 2 plus full stat equality,\n   with O_EXCL fallback where hardlinks are unsupported — plus an explicit LUSTRE INSANITY CHECK\n   (\"link has been observed to incorrectly *succeed*, despite the dest already existing\"), detected by\n   scanning the directory for two entries with the same name (devblog day 337).\n - CRITICALLY: linkToLock Nothing _ _ = return Nothing — WITHOUT THE SIDE LOCK IT WILL NOT EVEN\n   ATTEMPT ACQUISITION. The side lock is a PRECONDITION, not just a diagnostic.\nRETRY POLICY: FLAT 1-SECOND POLL, NO EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF, HARD ABORT. waitLock: \"Uses a 1 second\nwait-loop, retrying until a timeout. After the first second waiting, runs the callback to display a\nmessage, so the user knows why it's stalled\" -> \"waiting for pid lock file ... which is held by\nanother process (or may be stale)\", then at exhaustion \"Gave up waiting for pid lock file ...\".\nTWO-TIER LOCKING (Utility/LockPool/PidLock.hs L34-43): \"There are two levels of locks. A STM lock is\nused to handle fine-grained locking among threads... The pid lock handles locking between processes.\"\nSELF-DEADLOCK ACROSS SUBPROCESSES is real and handled out-of-band: one pid lock per repo means a\nprocess holding it that spawns git, which invokes `git-annex filter-process`, deadlocks against\nitself. Fix is an env var PIDLOCK_<mangled-abs-path>=1 signalling \"your parent holds it\"\n(PidLock.hs L322-336), applied AD HOC — commit 0485dd316 explains why not globally: \"it would need to\ntake the pid lock when running any and all processes, and that would be a problem when git-annex runs\ntwo processes concurrently.\"\n\n2.3 libgit2, dulwich, JGit/Gerrit, gitoxide.\nLIBGIT2 (@ddf3b5c8): NO retry, NO timeout, NO config support. src/util/filebuf.c L43-51 is one\nexistence check + one O_CREAT|O_EXCL (futils.c L69-88), EEXIST -> GIT_ELOCKED. No loop, no sleep.\ngrep for filesRefLockTimeout|packedRefsTimeout -> ZERO HITS; libgit2 does NOT honour git's timeout\nconfigs. Index message (index.c L3858-3866): \"the index is locked; this might be due to a concurrent\nor crashed process\". GIT_ELOCKED = -14. The de-facto contract is in libgit2's OWN TESTS —\ntests/libgit2/threads/refdb.c L45-93 wraps every call in `do { ... } while (error == GIT_ELOCKED);`\n— AN UNBOUNDED BUSY-SPIN WITH NO SLEEP, a model to note, not to copy. Maintainer position on stale\nlocks (libgit2#809): cleanup deferred to the APPLICATION, with user approval. Live NFS contention bug:\nlibgit2#6858 (open since Aug 2024, no maintainer response). Windows-only retry (do_with_retries,\nposix_w32.c L160-197) is for AV/sharing violations, not .lock contention.\nDULWICH (@d1c5d905): same, simpler. dulwich/file.py L210-234 — one os.open(O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_EXCL),\nFileExistsError -> FileLocked. No retry, no sleep, and GitFile() HAS NO TIMEOUT PARAMETER TO PASS.\nZero hits for the git timeout config names. FileLocked is raised and NEVER CAUGHT ANYWHERE in\ndulwich. The close() docstring names the hazard: \"If this method fails, it will attempt to delete the\nlockfile. However, it is not guaranteed to do so... which will prevent future writes to this file\nuntil the lockfile is removed manually.\"\nJGIT (@3f3beede) — PARTIAL. LockFile.createLockFileWithRetry() is MISLEADINGLY NAMED: one retry, only\nfor NoSuchFileException (racing mkdirs), NOT for contention. The only contention backoff is\npacked-refs-only and HARDCODED — RefDirectory.java L123-125: RETRY_SLEEP_MS = (0,100,200,400,800,1600)\n(~3.1s, six attempts, then LockFailedException); the setter is package-private, NOT\nOPERATOR-CONFIGURABLE. Loose ref locks get nothing — RefUpdate returns Result.LOCK_FAILURE\nimmediately. Zero hits for filesRefLockTimeout/packedRefsTimeout. JGit DOES add an in-process fair\nqueue, and the comment is the best statement of the two-tier idea found (RefDirectory.java L157-171):\n\"This lock exists so multiple threads in the same process can wait in a fair queue without trying,\nfailing, and retrying to acquire the on-disk lock.\"\nGERRIT — RETRIES THE OPERATION, NOT THE LOCK. THE MOST TRANSFERABLE PATTERN.\nExceptionHookImpl.java L34-58: shouldRetry(...) { return isLockFailure(throwable); }. Consumed by\nRetryHelper with WaitStrategies.join(exponentialWait(maxWait=5s), randomWait(50ms)) under a 20s total\nbudget, documented as retry.maxWait / retry.timeout in config-gerrit.txt: \"Maximum time to wait\nbetween attempts to retry an operation when one attempt fails (e.g. on NoteDb updates due to\ncontention, aka lock failure, on the underlying ref storage). Operations are retried with exponential\nbackoff, plus some random jitter... Defaults to 5 seconds.\"\nGerrit preserves the causal chain so the retry can fire — MergeOp.java:1076: \"Lock failures are a\nspecial case: RetryHelper depends on this specific causal chain in order to trigger a retry.\"\nRETRYING THE WHOLE LOGICAL OP IS WHAT MAKES IT SAFE: it re-reads base state.\nGITOXIDE (@b95db7ca) is THE ONLY NON-C IMPLEMENTATION AT PARITY. gix-lock/src/acquire.rs L13-20:\n  pub enum Fail { #[default] Immediately, AfterDurationWithBackoff(Duration) }\n  /// Retry after failure with quadratically longer sleep times to block the current thread.\nquadratic-with-jitter backoff, error PermanentlyLocked { resource_path, mode, attempts } hinting \"The\nlockfile ... might need manual deletion\"; and it HONOURS BOTH GIT KEYS WITH GIT'S DEFAULTS\n(gix/src/config/cache/access.rs L220-238).\nSCOREBOARD: gitoxide = PARITY; JGit = PARTIAL + HARDCODED; libgit2 & dulwich = NOTHING.\n\n2.4 AVOIDANCE PATTERNS — DON'T TAKE THE LOCK.\nGIT_INDEX_FILE PER OPERATION. git(1): \"This environment variable specifies an alternate index file.\"\nGit's own canonical user is `git stash` — builtin/stash.c L502-521 states the reason verbatim:\n  /* We need to run restore files from a given index, but without affecting the current index, so we\n     use GIT_INDEX_FILE with run_command to fork processes that will not interfere. */\nplus in-process equivalents write_index_as_tree(..., stash_index_path.buf, ...),\ncreate_index_from_tree(), set_alternate_index_output(). `git filter-branch` does it globally\n(git-filter-branch.sh:286: GIT_INDEX_FILE=\"$(pwd)/../index\"; export GIT_INDEX_FILE) and its docs teach\nusers the trick for --index-filter. git-annex uses it for its whole branch. libgit2's no-env\nequivalent is git_index_new() — \"Create an in-memory index object.\"\n*** WARNING: A PRIVATE INDEX REMOVES index.lock CONTENTION BUT NOT refs/**.lock CONTENTION — the final\nupdate-ref still hits the shared ref store. ***\nPURE PLUMBING, NO INDEX AT ALL: hash-object -w -> mktree (or scratch-index update-index --index-info +\nwrite-tree) -> commit-tree -> update-ref; only the last step locks, and only one ref. The cleanest\nin-tree proof is `git notes` — notes-utils.c L23-65 does write_notes_tree() -> commit_tree() ->\nrefs_update_ref(), CREATING NO index.lock EVER. Application exemplars: git-appraise (Google) stores\nreview data in git-notes under refs/notes/devtools, \"Each item stored is written as a single line of\nJSON\" — line-per-item PRECISELY SO CONCURRENT WRITES MERGE rather than conflict; GitHub's Git Data API\nis this model over HTTP (blobs->trees->commits->refs, base_tree for incremental builds, no checkout);\njosh rewrites whole histories via libgit2 treebuilders, with its single repo.index() call confined to\nthe explicit \"snapshot my dirty worktree\" input mode.\ngit worktree PARTITIONS THE INDEX, NOT THE REFS. git-worktree docs: each linked worktree gets its own\n\"files such as HEAD, index, etc.\", but \"In general, all pseudo refs are per-worktree and all refs\nstarting with refs/ are shared... SINCE REFS ARE SHARED ACROSS ALL WORKTREES, except refs/bisect,\nrefs/worktree and refs/rewritten.\" And \"By default, the repository config file is shared across all\nworktrees\" — so config.lock is shared too, which is exactly the bug core.configLockTimeout was written\nfor; df67d73ca: \"This shows up with parallel `git worktree add -b` against the same repository: each\none writes a couple of branch.* keys and the losers fail at random. Worse, `git worktree add` doesn't\npropagate that failure to its exit code, so the tracking config is silently dropped.\"\n*** NET: N WORKTREES => N INDEPENDENT index.lockS, BUT ONE refs/heads/<branch>.lock, ONE\npacked-refs.lock, ONE config.lock. ***\n\n== 3. WHERE THE ECOSYSTEM HAS NO SETTLED ANSWER — STATED EXPLICITLY ==\n1. index.lock RETRY. No knob, no proposal found, and NO UPSTREAM DOCUMENT EXPLAINING THE OMISSION. The\n   rationale must be INFERRED from adjacent ref-lock commits (f4ab4f3ab: single-writer semantics mean\n   a retry probably fails anyway) and from 27344d6a6's explicit rejection of inter-process\n   coordination as \"complicated and error-prone... maybe even impossible with just dotlocks.\" ANYONE\n   WHO TELLS YOU UPSTREAM HAS A STATED POSITION ON THIS IS OVER-READING.\n2. STALE-LOCK POLICY. Git has, deliberately, NONE for the general lockfile — and the 2026\n   core.lockfilePid work confirms the direction is BETTER DIAGNOSIS, STILL NO AUTO-BREAKING, with git\n   itself printing \"(PIDs can be reused)\". Meanwhile git-annex breaks locks aggressively but only\n   behind a side lock + hostname guard; libgit2 punts to the application \"with user approval\"; Gerrit\n   sidesteps by retrying the operation. FOUR MATURE PROJECTS, FOUR DIFFERENT ANSWERS.\n3. CROSS-HOST / CONTAINERISED STALENESS. git's gc.pid hostname scheme fails closed for 12h; the new\n   index~pid.lock records NO HOSTNAME AT ALL; git-annex's own docs say \"some networked filesystems are\n   so inconsistent that one node can't reliably tell when the other node is holding a pid lock. Caveat\n   emptor.\" The container-hostname failure mode for gc.pid is a straightforward reading of the source\n   but NO upstream bug report or acknowledgement was found — analysis, not citation.\n4. BACKOFF SHAPE. git = quadratic + jitter (cap 1s/sleep); gitoxide = same; Gerrit = exponential cap\n   5s + 50ms jitter, 20s budget; JGit = hardcoded 0/100/200/400/800/1600ms; git-annex = FLAT 1s POLL,\n   300s budget; libgit2's own tests = unbounded busy-spin. NO CONVERGENCE.\n\n== IF YOU ARE DESIGNING ON TOP OF THIS ==\nThe consistent signal across every mature implementation: REDUCE THE CONTENDED SURFACE RATHER THAN\nCONTEND BETTER. GIT_INDEX_FILE (or an in-memory index / pure plumbing) eliminates the index-lock class\noutright — git's own stash and filter-branch do it, git-annex does it, git-lfs does not and visibly\npays. What remains is one ref update, for which git DOES give you core.filesRefLockTimeout (100ms) and\ncore.packedRefsTimeout (1000ms), and for which GERRIT'S ANSWER — retry the idempotent LOGICAL\nOPERATION with jittered exponential backoff and a total budget, RE-READING BASE STATE each attempt —\nis the battle-tested shape. Add JGit's IN-PROCESS FAIR LOCK in front of the on-disk lock if you have\nintra-process concurrency (a few lines, removes self-thrash for free). And if you ever break a lock,\ndo it UNDER A PROVEN INVARIANT (git-annex's journal lock) or a CONSERVATIVE FAIL-CLOSED GUARD PLUS A\nWALL-CLOCK BACKSTOP (git's gc.pid), NEVER ON A TIMER ALONE.","timestamp":1785544953940002001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Progress on the top-2 recommendations from the cited research: (1) rec #1 (age ceiling on the refuse-without-proof _store/lock.py branches) landed as origin/main b1dd35483 under new bug 5389-1633-358e-44f9 (linked discovered_from here), closing the permanent-wedge CLASS behind the 2026-07-31 incident; _MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S=3600 backstop on the five no-liveness-signal branches, live-pid probe NOT overridden (never-on-a-timer-alone), holder+age WARNING on reclaim. (2) e43f (boot-path 60s lock budget) landed as 136aa4f15 — review-bot boot now uses a 5s×1 ensure-sweep budget behind the 30s HEALTH_TIMEOUT. Both merged with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1. Still open under this hub: c2ba (NEEDS DECISION — review-path concurrency model), e161 (CI flake, repro-first), 983f (BLOCKED on 01e8). Remaining research recs not yet done: #2 core.*Timeout knobs, #3 retry jitter, #4 watchdog subprocess timeouts, #6 macOS start-time fallback.","timestamp":1785554762758323001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"c2ba (review-bot shutdown/per-review timeouts unenforceable) is CLOSED — merged to main as 3aec0ae90a with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1 (Gerrit change 1148). The two unbounded residues that made the review-bot stop_grace_period unprovable are now bounded: (1) voter.py offloads the synchronous lock-held emit_code_review_artifact store write off the event loop via asyncio.to_thread, so the drain/per-review wait_for bounds can actually fire; (2) event_append.py routes all 13 lock-held git calls through a new _run_git() bounded by _GIT_TIMEOUT=30 (matching push.py), folding a hung git into a synthetic returncode-124 failure that unwinds cleanly out of write_lock. Defense-in-depth: app.py/config.py bound the lifespan post-drain cancel/await under a new SHUTDOWN_CANCEL_SECONDS (default 10). docker-compose stop_grace_period stays 150s but its comment is re-derived as a provable worst case rather than a risk reduction. This is rec #1's sibling on the write path; the age-ceiling (lock.py) and boot-budget (e43f-area) recs landed earlier this session. Remaining under this hub: e43f (boot-path lock budget), e161 (contention CI flake), 983f (network git call sites, BLOCKED on 01e8).","timestamp":1785559187093068001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"e161 (test_push_loop_converges_under_contention CI flake) is CLOSED (class=flaky) — merged to main as 0da317b2f with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1 (Gerrit change 1167). Root cause was NOT the classifier pattern nor an empty capture (the prior leading hypothesis): reconcile-bridge.yml:314 used 'echo \"$push_stderr\" | grep -qiE ...' under 'set -euo pipefail'. grep -q exits on its early match, and if echo is still writing the capture (large payload, or scheduler preemption on a loaded runner) echo takes SIGPIPE (141); pipefail then makes the pipeline non-zero, so a MATCHED retryable rejection is misclassified non-retryable and the bridge fails red. Confirmed by deterministic repro (2000/2000 misclassification with a 200KB early-match payload) and disproven empty-capture lead (the ELSE message shows the full stderr — the variable is fine, only the pipeline status is poisoned). Fix: here-string 'grep -qiE ... <<<\"$push_stderr\"' (no pipeline -> nothing to SIGPIPE, nothing for pipefail to poison; -iE semantics unchanged). New RED->GREEN test drives the extracted YAML block verbatim with a git shim that pads a real rejection's stderr; the existing small-stderr convergence test is the negative control. Remaining under this hub: e43f is already CLOSED; 983f (network git call sites) is BLOCKED on in-progress bug 01e8.","timestamp":1785600276283287001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"01e8 (init divergent-store) resolved and closed [plan_defect] — merged as Gerrit 1173 / origin main 4034e96388 (LLM-Review+1, Verified+1). Finding: the original symptom (single-branch clone bootstrapping a divergent empty store) NO LONGER reproduces on main — the landed explicit-refspec self-heal (5546/35f7) adopts origin/tickets (covered by test_sync_narrow_refspec). The one real remaining gap was AC3: rebar fsck mislabeled a genuinely divergent tracker as informational PUSH_PENDING / 'no issues found'. Fixed: fsck now classifies tracker-vs-origin using the same taxonomy as _store/sync.py and reports DIVERGED (no shared history, or non-fast-forwardable) as a COUNTED, non-zero issue naming the recovery path. This also delivers AC2's 'no silent data-hiding' intent via the health check; no init.py rearchitecture needed. Next: 983f is now UNBLOCKED — bounding the two _commands network git calls (init fetch, fsck_repair push).","timestamp":1785610112546465001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"983f (bound the two _commands network git calls) resolved and closed — merged as Gerrit 1174 / origin main 253224f354 (LLM-Review+1, Verified+1). Re-verified post-01e8 that neither call had been rewritten, so both were still unbounded; bounded by call profile: init.py cold fetch -> _FETCH_TIMEOUT=300 (repo_snapshot cold precedent, bug 747f); fsck_repair.py incremental push -> _PUSH_TIMEOUT=30 (_store/push.py precedent). Timeouts fold into a synthetic CompletedProcess(124) naming the op+bound (never a bare TimeoutExpired/hang), init surfacing an actionable WARNING and fsck_repair routing into its existing ABORT path. 7 held-out tests in test_git_timeout_bounds_983f.py.\n\n9305 cluster status this session: c2ba (review-bot timeout bounds) MERGED/closed; e161 (SIGPIPE push classifier) MERGED/closed; 01e8 (fsck DIVERGED detection) MERGED/closed; 983f (this) MERGED/closed. e43f was already closed. Remaining open under 9305: e161's sibling hypotheses are resolved; the hub itself and any residual local-only-callsite items can be reassessed — no parentless open bugs remain in this cluster that are outside the two in-progress epics' file impact.","timestamp":1785611975301662001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"OPERATOR-RATIFIED DECISION (2026-08-04) — recorded before implementation, per instruction.\n\nrebar must handle local git lock contention AUTOMATICALLY, with NO escalation to the agent\nrunning the command. Concretely:\n\n- A PER-STORE ADVISORY LOCK plus BOUNDED JITTERED RETRY/BACKOFF on the store's git call\n  sites, consistent with the existing push-retry pattern in rebar._store.push.\n- Transient lock conflicts (index.lock / ref locks briefly held by a peer) resolve SILENTLY —\n  at most a debug/warning log.\n- Only a genuinely stuck lock (bounded timeout exhausted) surfaces an actionable error naming\n  the lock file and holder guidance.\n- Bounded defaults on the order of tens of seconds total wait, jittered. Config-tunable ONLY\n  if a store/git config surface already exists — it does not (checked docs/config.md\n  inventory + src/rebar/config.py), so NO new config keys (inert/one-off config is a known\n  defect class here).\n\nOperator context: \"We've seen a lot of contention with parallel sessions\" — the commissioning\nsession itself observed a claim fail on a store lock and succeed on manual retry. (Also\nreproduced while working this ticket: `run_ensures: write lock unavailable, skipping sweep:\nflock: could not acquire lock after 60s` on a plain read command.)\n\nThis decision selects among the options the OSS research (comments above) left open: bounded\njittered retry (git lockfile.c's jittered backoff shape, Mercurial's bounded wait) + own-lock\nserialization (JGit's in-process fair lock before the file lock; git-annex's single-flusher\nrule), rather than block-forever (cargo/Bazel) or fail-fast. Research recs #1 (age ceiling)\nlanded separately as 5389; rec #4 (jitter on the store-lock sleeps) is folded into this\nticket's implementation.\n\nSTRICT TDD is an operator condition for this bug class: RED behavioral tests first —\n(a) a git op hitting a transient index.lock held briefly by another process succeeds after\nretry with no error surfaced; (b) a permanently held lock produces one actionable error after\nthe bounded timeout; (c) uncontended ops have no added latency (no sleep on the happy path).","timestamp":1785862959509991001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Implementation pushed as Gerrit change 1376 (Change-Id If7bd2a62a5b01b39d6d57ba8208981e10fb5994e, patchset 1, commit e65c5e0ea5).\n\nTDD trail: RED behavioral suite tests/interfaces/store/test_git_contention_9305.py written and confirmed failing first (a transient index.lock held ~3s by a competing process hard-failed the write under the old ~2s budget), then the seam changes turned it green:\n\n- gitutil._store_git_op_lock: per-store advisory flock (rebar-git-op.lock in the tracker git dir) held across each _with_index_lock_retry run — non-blocking uncontended (test: zero sleeps on the happy path), kernel-released on death, bounded jittered wait (30s) then proceed-without at warning (advisory; git-level retry governs).\n- _with_index_lock_retry: jittered (uniform 0.75-1.25x) exponential backoff capped 5s, 10 attempts (~21s nominal budget — _lock_retry_budget_s pinned to [10s,60s] by test); ref-lock signature (cannot lock ref / *.lock File exists) now retried too; stale reclamation stays index.lock-only; exhaustion folds actionable guidance into stderr naming the contended lock file + remedy.\n- lock.py: jittered the 50ms/100ms contention sleeps (research rec 4).\n- Watchdogs (AC: bounded-or-documented per site): run_git_write 120s rc-124 fold (txn/compact/delete); fsck both read sites 120s; metrics blame + git_metrics 300s (TimeoutExpired -> existing best-effort None / per-metric unavailable via _commands/metrics.py fault isolation); event_append keeps its 30s.\n- compact.py: the two failure branches that discarded cp.stderr now include it so the exhaustion guidance reaches the operator.\n\nValidation: new suite 9/9 green; related regression suites (interfaces/store, compact/delete index-lock, run_git wrapper, git-timeout bounds, raw-git lint, store concurrency) 387 passed / 1 failed — the 1 failure (test_tracker_config_e2e::test_custom_dir_and_branch_init_and_sync_roundtrip) reproduces on UNMODIFIED origin/main f70e5d2101 in this env, pre-existing and unrelated. make lint + make typecheck exit zero. Awaiting LLM-Review +1 / Verified +1.","timestamp":1785865020800190001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"CI remediation for Gerrit change 1376 — pushed patch set 3 (78e1dd8816, same Change-Id If7bd2a6). Fixes for the PS2 Verified failures:\n\n1) lint (lint + type-check job): comment-hygiene gate failed — src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py:1 module docstring tripped [dated-incident] on '2026-08-04' + 'observed'. Fixed by dropping the rot-prone date and citing the durable grouped-hex ticket id: '(bug 9305, operator-ratified 2026-08-04)' -> '(bug 9305-b42c, operator-ratified)'. ruff check/format, mypy, config-check, verify-gate-parity all green.\n\n2) pytest (ubuntu/macos py3.11-3.13): the ~341 reconciler BackendEnvError failures were STALE — they came from an older tree (the error strings 'missing Jira Cloud configuration' / HTTP-Basic-auth cloud message do not exist anywhere in the PS2 tree). PS2's actual tree passes them locally, incl. the exact CI-named tests (test_applier, test_managed_refs unlink-after-compaction, test_mcp readonly dry-run, test_code_review_artifact). Root cause: origin/main had advanced 4 commits past PS2's base (a41705); rebased PS2 onto current origin/main so the fresh CI merge-preview reflects current main.\n\nLocal status on the rebased PS3 tree: make lint / typecheck / config-check GREEN; git-lock behavioral suite tests/interfaces/store/test_git_contention_9305.py 9/9 pass; reconciler apply + previously-CI-failing suites 215/215 pass. (Pre-existing host-env failures ignored: ddgs ImportError in test_web_tool_gating, plus test_ref_lock/reconcile_bridge push-retry which also fail on pristine origin/main.) Change intent unchanged (local git call sites absorb lock contention). Awaiting fresh LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1 before submit; ticket stays in_progress.","timestamp":1785871813684555001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"CI remediation round 2 -> pushed patch set 4 to Gerrit change 1376 (Change-Id If7bd2a6...). \n\nPS3 Verified-1 had two failing jobs:\n1. pytest (ubuntu, py3.12): test_git_contention_9305.py::test_uncontended_write_never_sleeps failed - asserted zero sleeps on the uncontended happy path but observed [0.001]. Root cause: run_git_write now passes timeout=_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT to subprocess.run; on POSIX, stdlib Popen._wait(timeout) busy-polls with time.sleep(~0.001) when the child-reap race hits (common on loaded CI runners). The test's GLOBAL time.sleep monkeypatch mis-counted that stdlib internal sleep as rebar-added latency. Fix: routed rebar's three deliberate backoff sleeps through a new gitutil._backoff_sleep seam and made the test patch that seam, so it measures ONLY rebar's intentional backoff (behavior unchanged). All 9 contention tests pass locally.\n2. verify-identity: 'tickets pack is 131431 KiB; limit is 131072 KiB' - GLOBAL tickets-store pack-size limit, not code in this change; will fail ANY change until an operator compacts the tickets pack. Not fixable here.\n\nAlso rebased onto current origin/main (was 7 commits behind) to avoid a stale-merge-preview artifact. Local: make lint PASS, make typecheck PASS (430 files), targeted contention suite 9/9. Note: tests/interfaces/store/test_tracker_config_e2e.py::test_custom_dir_and_branch_init_and_sync_roundtrip fails identically on pristine origin/main on this host (for-each-ref exit 128) - pre-existing/host env, not this change. Leaving 9305 in_progress pending Verified+1 / merge.","timestamp":1785874032153514001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Landed as Gerrit 1376 / commit 42076d367e (\"store: absorb local git lock contention (9305)\"),\nMERGED (LLM-Review +1 + Verified +1 on PS4) and reachable from origin/main. AC verification:\n\nAC1 — OSS research findings recorded: 8 research-bearing comments on this ticket; the Approach\n  cites them. ✓\nAC2 — transient index.lock/ref-lock self-heals: covered by the contention suite (behavioral (a)\n  + ref-lock test). ✓\nAC3 — permanently held lock -> one actionable error naming the lock file + remedy, bounded\n  jittered budget: behavioral test (b). ✓\nAC4 — uncontended writes add no latency (no happy-path sleep): behavioral test (c). This was the\n  PS3 flake (stdlib subprocess internal sleep mis-counted); fixed in PS4 via the gitutil\n  _backoff_sleep seam so the test measures only rebar's deliberate backoff. ✓\nAC5 — every local git call site bounded-with-watchdog or documented-unbounded, incl. BOTH fsck\n  sites (fsck.py:50 and :400, each annotated \"Watchdog, not a latency budget (9305)\"). ✓\nAC6 — blocking policy stated in gitutil.py module docstring (bounded jittered retry + advisory\n  serialization; silent transient recovery; one actionable error on a stuck lock). ✓\nAC7 — make lint / make typecheck / targeted tests exit zero: green in CI (Verified +1) and\n  re-run locally now — tests/interfaces/store/test_git_contention_9305.py -> 9 passed. ✓\nVerdict: all ACs satisfied. Closing.","timestamp":1785876339880290001},{"author":"reconciler","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"OSS RESEARCH — LOCAL CONTENTION HANDLING IN MATURE PROJECTS (commissioned 2026-07-31)\n\n>>> CORRECTION BY THE COORDINATOR, READ FIRST <<<\nThe report's \"Grounding note\" claims commit 5ef138d26 (the 304e boot-id fix) is NOT reachable from main and that lock.py still carries the pre-fix `_owner_stamp()`. THAT IS WRONG. The researcher measured against a STALE LOCAL CHECKOUT (HEAD 2a3abe6ab) instead of origin/main. Verified by me: `git merge-base --is-ancestor 5ef138d26 origin/main` => TRUE; origin/main's _store/lock.py contains the v2 stamp (`rebar-lock v2 host=... ns=... pid=... start=...`, _STAMP_V2_PREFIX at :177). CONSEQUENCE: priority item #2 in section 5d (\"Land fix-304e-container-lock on main\") is ALREADY DONE — ignore it. Everything else in the report stands. (This is the same stale-worktree trap that bit me earlier tonight: always read `git show origin/main:<path>`, never the working tree.)\n\nEvery section separates CITED (a URL backs it) from EXTRAPOLATION (inference).\n\n== 1. LOCAL GIT CONTENTION (index.lock / HEAD.lock / packed-refs.lock) ==\n\n1a. What git gives you — CITED. Git HAS retry-with-backoff and deliberately does NOT use it for the index. Generic path `lock_file_timeout()` in lockfile.c: quadratic backoff (multiplier += 2n+1, cap BACKOFF_MAX_MULTIPLIER 1000, base INITIAL_BACKOFF_MS 1) with EXPLICIT JITTER — `wait_ms = (750 + rand() % 500) * backoff_ms / 1000`, commented \"back off for between 0.75*backoff_ms and 1.25*backoff_ms\". Retries only on EEXIST. timeout_ms==0 => one attempt; -1 => forever. THE INDEX TAKES THE ZERO-TIMEOUT PATH: repo_hold_locked_index() takes no timeout and calls repo_hold_lock_file_for_update(), the wrapper passing timeout_ms=0 (repository.c L471-478, lockfile.h L223-230). So .git/index.lock = one attempt, no retry, NO CONFIG KNOB. The three knobs that exist (Documentation/config/core.adoc L580-598): core.filesRefLockTimeout (default 100ms), core.packedRefsTimeout (default 1000ms), core.configLockTimeout (default 1000ms). 0 = no retry, -1 = indefinite. No index equivalent. Why refs got retry: Michael Haggerty's thread \"Retry acquiring reference locks for 100ms\" — pack-refs and reflog expire lock refs WITHOUT changing their values, so retry has a real chance; GitHub saw ~10-50 such errors/day fleet-wide (~1 in a million updates), near-zero after retry. Jeff King argued packed-refs needs a longer timeout since it is O(number of refs). The thread never discusses an index.lock equivalent. New on git master (post-2025): core.lockfilePid writes a sibling foo~pid.lock containing \"pid <n>\", PURELY DIAGNOSTIC. On failed acquire, unable_to_lock_message() probes kill(pid,0) and emits an honest hedge: \"if no git process is running, the lock file may be stale (PIDs can be reused)\". Adds diagnosis, not retry. EXTRAPOLATION: found no message explicitly REJECTING an index-lock timeout knob; the absence is evidenced by source/docs, not a stated decision. Plausible reason: index.lock is held across arbitrary user work (conflicted merge, interactive add) where \"wait longer\" is not a meaningful bet, whereas ref locks are held for microseconds by maintenance.\n\n1b. Do tools set the knobs? — CITED. YES, the largest one does. Gitaly forces both globally on every git invocation, reasoning inline (internal/git/gitcmd/command_factory.go L752-763):\n  \"Git has a default timeout of 1 second to try and lock the file. In practice though we see that\n   this is not sufficient, and especially the DeleteRefs RPC is erroring out very frequently. We thus\n   increase the timeout to 10 seconds. While comparatively high, context cancellation would still\n   cause us to exit early in case the caller doesn't want to wait this long.\"\n  core.packedRefsTimeout = 10000; core.filesRefLockTimeout = 1000 (\"we have still observed lock\n  contention around them though, but mostly in cases where the host system was heavily loaded by a\n  storm of incoming RPCs\"). Driving issue: gitaly#5160.\nEXTRAPOLATION: outside Gitaly this appears rare — no other project found doing it. It is cheap (`git -c core.packedRefsTimeout=...`) and UNDER-USED.\n\n1c. The four mitigations — CITED.\n  (a) Retry with jittered backoff: git lockfile.c; JGit RETRY_SLEEP_MS = [0,100,200,400,800,1600]\n      ~3.1s for packed-refs (RefDirectory.java); lazygit 5x50ms then forcible unlink (commit 718e6d3).\n  (b) Serialize behind your own lock: JGit holds a FAIR in-process ReentrantLock\n      (inProcessPackedRefsLock = new ReentrantLock(true)) BEFORE touching the file lock; git-annex's\n      joeyh fixed a user's index.lock failures by \"only allow one git queue to be flushed at a time\".\n  (c) GIT_INDEX_FILE / worktree isolation: git-annex withIndexFile -> .git/annex/index\n      (Git/Index.hs, Annex/GitOverlay.hs); GIT ITSELF does it in `git stash`\n      (GIT_INDEX_FILE=$stash_index_path, builtin/stash.c) and git-filter-branch.sh L286-287.\n      GOTCHA git-annex documents: the path MUST BE ABSOLUTE — a relative one resolves against the\n      worktree top and is perturbed by GIT_WORK_TREE/core.worktree.\n  (d) Avoid the index (plumbing): git-lfs's hot path (clean/smudge/filter-process) touches no index —\n      a code search for index.lock across git-lfs/git-lfs returns ZERO hits; index use is confined to\n      `git update-index -q --refresh` in git/git.go. Gerrit runs bare through JGit\n      ObjectInserter/BatchRefUpdate, never a DirCache.\nBEST-ARGUED STATEMENT OF THE PATTERN — git-annex prepareModifyIndex (Annex/Branch.hs L387-412), which deletes any .git/annex/index.lock it finds, justified as: \"Since all modifications of the index are performed inside this module, and only when the journal is locked... no other process is currently modifying the index. So any index.lock file must be stale.\" => THE LICENCE CONDITION for unlinking a git lock: OWN THE INDEX FILE EXCLUSIVELY **AND** HOLD YOUR OWN LOCK OVER IT. Absent that, blind unlink is unsafe. lazygit's unconditional os.Remove and GitLab Runner's delete-before-checkout are UI/CI shortcuts assuming single-writer. NOTABLE NEGATIVE RESULTS — CITED: dulwich does exactly one O_EXCL open and converts FileExistsError straight into FileLocked — no loop, no timeout, no staleness check (dulwich/file.py L168-283). libgit2's filebuf.c lock_file() returns GIT_ELOCKED immediately with no retry and implements NONE of git's ref-lock timeout semantics (filebuf.c L44-58) — so LIBGIT2-BASED TOOLS ARE MORE LOCK-FRAGILE THAN THE C GIT CLI EVEN ON REFS. JGit's DirCache.lock() mirrors C git and does not retry the index.\n\n== 2. TIMEOUTS ON PURELY LOCAL GIT SUBPROCESS CALLS — NO SETTLED ANSWER == CITED FOR: Gitaly bounds EVERY git command with a Go context deadline, in the same breath as raising the lock timeouts — \"context cancellation would still cause us to exit early in case the caller doesn't want to wait this long\". GitLab layers per-request Gitaly timeouts above that (MR 224510). CITED AGAINST (the observed limit of the practice): where projects added git timeouts they scoped them to NETWORK ops. Zuul's \"Add git timeout\" bounds remote clone/fetch at 300s and DELETES THE LOCAL REPO on timeout, explicitly because GitPython's clone_from()/fetch() lack hard timeouts; local operations untouched. GitPython's reported hangs (#547, #969) are clone/fetch. dulwich, libgit2, git-lfs and git-annex bound NOTHING at the subprocess level. EXTRAPOLATION — the honest synthesis: bounding a purely local git call is UNUSUAL IN APPLICATION CODE AND STANDARD IN SERVER INFRASTRUCTURE. The dividing line is not local-vs-network, it is \"IS THERE A CALLER WITH A DEADLINE?\" Gitaly has one (an RPC); a CLI does not.\n  FOR: an unbounded local call can stall indefinitely on a stuck peer lock, a hung NFS/FUSE/network\n  mount, or a wedged filesystem — and rebar's calls run WHILE HOLDING THE STORE WRITE LOCK, so one\n  stall converts into a fleet-wide stall of every other agent. Failure without a bound is\n  indistinguishable from slowness.\n  AGAINST: a timeout can abort legitimately slow work (large gc, repack, cold-cache add on a huge\n  tree) and, worse, can abort it MID-WRITE — which for git means leaving the very .lock files you\n  were trying to avoid. Zuul's mitigation (delete and re-clone) is NOT available to rebar's tracker.\nRECONCILABLE POSITION: a WATCHDOG-GRADE bound (generous, 2-10x worst legitimate case) is defensible; a LATENCY-GRADE bound is not.\n\n== 3. STALE-LOCK RECLAMATION: FOUR SCHEMES == THE DIRECT PRIOR ART — MERCURIAL — CITED. mercurial/lock.py already contains rebar's 304e fix:\n  def _getlockprefix():\n      \"\"\"Return a string which is used to differentiate pid namespaces\n      It's useful to detect \"dead\" processes and remove stale locks with confidence. Typically it's\n      just hostname. On modern linux, we include an extra Linux-specific pid namespace identifier.\"\"\"\n      result = encoding.strtolocal(socket.gethostname())\n      if pycompat.sysplatform.startswith(b'linux'):\n          try: result += b'/%x' % os.stat(b'/proc/self/ns/pid').st_ino\n          except (FileNotFoundError, PermissionError, NotADirectoryError): pass\n      return result\nFOUR DESIGN POINTS WORTH STEALING, same file:\n 1. THE LOCK IS A SYMLINK WHOSE TARGET IS THE STAMP — creation and content are ONE ATOMIC STEP:\n    \"lock is symlink on platforms that support it, file on others. symlink is used because create of\n    directory entry and contents are atomic even over nfs. old-style lock: symlink to pid.\n    new-style lock: symlink to hostname:pid\"\n 2. BREAKING A STALE LOCK IS ITSELF DONE UNDER A SECOND LOCK. _testlock(): \"if locker dead, break\n    lock. must do this with another lock held, or can race and break valid lock\" — takes <name>.break\n    with timeout=0, RE-READS THE STAMP, and only then unlinks.\n 3. FOREIGN PREFIX => NEVER BREAK (_lockshouldbebroken: if host != lock._host: return False).\n 4. BOUNDED WAIT WITH A WARNING STAGE: lock() sleeps 1s per attempt down from ui.timeout, DEFAULT\n    600s, with ui.timeout.warn (default 0) driving \"waiting for lock on ... held by ...\" and a\n    \"got lock after %d seconds\" follow-up (configitems.toml).\nWHAT MERCURIAL DOES NOT HAVE: no boot id, no process start-time qualification, NO AGE CEILING. THE CONTAINER FAILURE MODE, BOTH DIRECTIONS — CITED:\n - rebar's direction (same host misread as foreign => orphan permanent): hostname is the container id\n   and changes on recreate. rebar 5ef138d26 documents field data: 4f2e3e889406 -> 3790c7a6034a while\n   boot id identical.\n - THE INVERSE, MORE DANGEROUS DIRECTION (foreign misread as stale => a LIVE lock broken):\n   restic#4922 — running restic inside Flatpak, \"when considering whether a lock from a restic\n   process outside the container is stale or not, it (correctly) sees the same hostname but then\n   can't see the pid and always decides the lock is stale.\" Same hostname, different PID namespace.\n   THIS IS THE CASE rebar's `ns=` FIELD GUARDS.\n - boot_id IS GENUINELY HOST-WIDE, NOT NAMESPACED — the request to containerize it was closed\n   WONTFIX, Lennart Poettering: \"PID namespaces have many users, beyond systems which try to boot-up\n   systems in them... Overmounting the boot id in userspace is very easy\" (RH bug 854604).\n   Consequences: (i) boot_id is the right host identity — CITED; (ii) trivially spoofable by an\n   overmount and absent on non-Linux — CITED for mechanism, EXTRAPOLATION for risk.\nCOMPARISON:\n - pid + start-time: sound on one host+namespace. Footguns: starttime is clock-tick granular\n   (/proc/pid/stat field 22); unavailable on macOS/Windows (degrades to bare pid); meaningless across\n   pid namespaces. Kernel devs call starttime \"useful to detect PID reuses in a somewhat reliable\n   way\" but tick-granular (LKML). filelock implements it: \"A PID that no longer exists is gone; a\n   live PID whose start token differs is a recycled PID\".\n - KERNEL flock/fcntl: SOUNDEST — auto-released on death, no reclamation logic to get wrong.\n   Footguns: advisory only; broken/absent on NFS; POSIX fcntl locks are LOST ON ANY close() of the\n   fd, hence OFD locks. cargo SKIPS LOCKING ENTIRELY ON NFS because \"the failure mode for flock on\n   NFS is BLOCKING FOREVER, even if the 'non-blocking' flag is passed\" (flock.rs). Bazel prefers OFD\n   locks — \"POSIX locks can be lost 'accidentally' due to any close() on the lock file, and are not\n   reliably preserved across execve()\" (blaze_util_posix.cc).\n - LEASE/TTL + HEARTBEAT: sound, and THE ONLY SCHEME THAT CANNOT WEDGE FOREVER. Footguns: needs clock\n   sync; a paused/swapped holder can have its live lock stolen. restic: staleLockTimeout = 30min,\n   refreshed every 5min, refreshabilityTimeout = staleLockTimeout - defaultRefreshInterval*3/2 \"to\n   compensate for a small time drift between clients\". proper-lockfile (yarn 1): mkdir strategy,\n   stale 10s, mtime heartbeat every stale/2, and it AVOIDS O_EXCL because \"O_EXCL is broken on NFS\n   file systems\". flufl.lock has explicit lifetime + refresh.\n - BOOT-ID QUALIFICATION: sound for HOST identity, strictly better than hostname. Linux-only;\n   spoofable by overmount; does NOT identify the namespace (needs the ns= companion).\nTHE HYBRID GIT ITSELF USES, THAT NOBODY NAMES: `git gc`'s gc.pid combines hostname + pid PLUS A GENEROUS TTL BACKSTOP, in lock_repo_for_gc() (builtin/gc.c L718-778):\n  /* 12 hour limit is very generous as gc should never take that long. On the other hand we don't\n     really need a strict limit here, running gc --auto one day late is not a big problem. --force\n     can be used in manual gc after the user verifies that no gc is running. */\n  time(NULL) - st.st_mtime <= 12 * 3600 &&\n  fscanf(fp, scan_fmt, &pid, locking_host) == 2 &&\n  /* be gentle to concurrent \"gc\" on remote hosts */\n  (strcmp(locking_host, my_host) || !kill(pid, 0) || errno == EPERM);\nREAD CAREFULLY: a FOREIGN HOST'S STAMP IS UNCONDITIONALLY RESPECTED (the conservative rule rebar shares) — BUT the 12-hour mtime ceiling means it can never wedge the repository FOREVER. *** THAT COMBINATION IS THE PIECE REBAR IS MISSING. *** Yarn Berry states the asymmetry that makes liveness probes safe: \"It's not foolproof (there are false positive), but there are no false negative and that's all that matters in 99% of the cases\" — a false \"alive\" makes you wait (safe); a false \"dead\" makes you break a live lock (corruption). Bazel's rule is the cleanest risk-elimination: it writes pid/owner/cwd/tty into the lock and comments \"The contents are printed for human consumption when another client fails to take the lock, but not parsed otherwise\" — ALL THE DIAGNOSTIC VALUE OF AN OWNER STAMP, ZERO PID-REUSE EXPOSURE, because the kernel lock is the only authority.\n\n== 4. BLOCKING POLICY UNDER CONTENTION, AND THE STARTUP CASE == WHAT MATURE TOOLS DO — CITED:\n cargo package cache: try_lock then KERNEL-BLOCK FOREVER; prints \"Blocking waiting for file lock on\n   package cache\" once. No timeout.\n Bazel client: 500ms poll loop FOREVER; prints owner dump then \"Waiting for it to complete...\";\n   --noblock_for_lock => exit LOCK_HELD_NOBLOCK_FOR_LOCK. NO --lock_timeout EXISTS (0 code hits).\n Bazel server: commandLock.wait(500) loop forever; LockingMode.ERROR_OUT escape.\n Mercurial: 1s poll to ui.timeout = 600s, with a warn stage; timeout=0 escape.\n Python filelock: poll, timeout=-1 = forever; multi-file backends use JITTERED EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF\n   with secrets.randbelow; timeout=0 = one attempt.\n portalocker: BOUNDED — DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5s, DEFAULT_CHECK_INTERVAL = 0.25s, then raise.\n yarn 1: 200ms poll FOREVER, --mutex opt-in. yarn berry: 16.7ms poll, HARD 60s then throw.\n pnpm: NO STORE LOCK AT ALL. git (ref locks): jittered quadratic backoff to a config'd budget.\n git gc --auto: FAIL FAST, SILENTLY.\nPNPM'S ANSWER IS THE MOST RADICAL AND IT WORKS. zkochan, pnpm discussion #10702: \"It is safe to run multiple installs concurrently, which modify the same store. All store related operations are atomic. The store will never be left in a broken state. The store is not locked. When multiple installers use the same store, they might redundantly download and extract the same package multiple times but it will work.\" Contention DESIGNED OUT via atomic rename-based writes; the trade is redundant work. (Could not confirm the PR that removed the old directory locking — \"pnpm v6\" is UNVERIFIED.) NO SETTLED ANSWER: block-forever (cargo, Bazel, yarn 1) and bounded (portalocker, berry, Mercurial, git) are both mainstream. Near-universal among block-forever: A VISIBLE, REPEATED WAITING MESSAGE NAMING THE HOLDER. Near-universal among bounded: JITTER. THE STARTUP CASE — CITED. Git's own answer to \"a background/implicit path wants a lock\" is FAIL FAST, SILENTLY (builtin/gc.c L992-1001):\n  name = lock_repo_for_gc(force, &pid);\n  if (name) {\n          if (opts.auto_flag) { ret = 0; goto out; /* be quiet on --auto */ }\n          die(_(\"gc is already running on machine '%s' pid %\"PRIuMAX\" (use --force if not)\"), ...);\n  }\nAn EXPLICIT `git gc` dies loudly with the holder's host and pid. The IMPLICIT --auto path — the one on every user's hot path, EXACTLY ANALOGOUS TO REBAR'S BOOT ENSURE SWEEP — returns success and does nothing. Housekeeping is opportunistic; it never blocks the thing the user asked for. Bazel's --noblock_for_lock docs make the same point for automation: \"Developers might use this in presubmit checks to avoid long waits caused by another Bazel command in the same client.\" EXTRAPOLATION — the general rule: THE ACQUISITION BUDGET MUST BE STRICTLY SMALLER THAN THE DEADLINE OF WHOEVER IS WAITING ON YOU. rebar's incident was a 60s budget under a 30s health check — a straightforward violation. COROLLARY THAT MATTERS MORE: fail-fast on a startup path must be LOUD IN TELEMETRY AND IDEMPOTENTLY RETRIED LATER, or you have converted a stall into silent skipped work.\n\n== 5. RECOMMENDATIONS FOR REBAR == 5a. DOES THE TWO-LEG flock+mkdir DESIGN MATCH, EXCEED, OR FALL SHORT? It EXCEEDS mature practice on soundness of reclamation and FALLS SHORT on liveness guarantees. WHERE IT EXCEEDS (EXTRAPOLATION grounded in the cited comparisons):\n - The fcntl leg gives the kernel-mediated auto-release property cargo and Bazel rely on. The mkdir\n   leg gives the portable second window and NFS tolerance flock lacks. Neither leg waits on the other\n   across processes, so no deadlock. VERY FEW PROJECTS RUN BOTH.\n - The 304e stamp (rebar-lock v2 host=<boot-id|name> ns=<pidns-inode> pid=<pid> start=<starttime>) is\n   STRICTLY STRONGER THAN MERCURIAL'S hostname/nsinode:pid — it adds boot-id host identity and\n   start-time pid qualification, NEITHER OF WHICH MERCURIAL HAS.\n - The \"reclaim only under the fcntl proof\" rule for the cross-namespace case is GENUINELY NOVEL and,\n   as far as the researcher could tell, correct. NO PRIOR ART FOUND DOING THIS. Sounder than the\n   alternatives (mtime heuristic, or refusing forever).\n - rebar's colon-free v2 stamp, deliberately unparseable to an older reader, is BETTER\n   FORWARD-COMPATIBILITY HYGIENE than anything in the surveyed projects.\nWHERE IT FALLS SHORT:\n 1. NO AGE CEILING ANYWHERE. git gc has 12h, restic 30min, proper-lockfile 10s, flufl.lock a lifetime.\n    rebar has none. EVERY \"refuse without proof\" branch — absent stamp, malformed stamp, foreign host\n    identity — WEDGES THE STORE PERMANENTLY. That is the CLASS of the 304e incident; 304e fixed one\n    instance (the container-recreate identity) and LEFT THE CLASS OPEN.\n 2. NO JITTER. _acquire_fcntl sleeps a fixed 50ms, _acquire_mkdir a fixed 100ms, and\n    gitutil._with_index_lock_retry a linear 0.2 x attempt. Git jitters +-25% SPECIFICALLY to\n    de-synchronize competing processes. With many agents this is a THUNDERING HERD.\n 3. NO HOLDER DISCLOSURE TO THE WAITER. cargo, Bazel, Mercurial and yarn all tell the user who holds\n    the lock and that they are waiting. rebar waits SILENTLY for up to 60s then reports only a\n    duration. Bazel's \"print it, never parse it\" is free to adopt.\n5b. SHOULD LOCAL-ONLY GIT CALL SITES BE BOUNDED? YES, BUT AS A WATCHDOG, NOT A LATENCY BUDGET.\n 1. SET GIT'S THREE LOCK KNOBS ON EVERY INVOCATION — highest value per unit risk, and the only\n    fully cited-precedent move (Gitaly). Add\n    `-c core.filesRefLockTimeout=1000 -c core.packedRefsTimeout=10000 -c core.configLockTimeout=10000`\n    to the module-local _git helpers. Bounds HEAD.lock / packed-refs.lock / config.lock contention\n    INSIDE GIT where it belongs, and costs nothing when uncontended.\n 2. ADD A GENEROUS subprocess.run(..., timeout=...) WATCHDOG — 120s for ordinary plumbing, higher for\n    compact/fsck/gc-adjacent work. rebar's calls run WHILE HOLDING THE STORE WRITE LOCK, so an\n    unbounded stall on a hung mount converts one process's problem into every agent's — precisely the\n    coupling Gitaly's context deadlines prevent. Keep it well above any legitimate duration; the goal\n    is to distinguish \"wedged\" from \"slow\". EXTRAPOLATION: treat a timeout as a HARD FAULT (surface\n    it, release the lock) rather than retrying — an aborted git write is exactly what leaves .lock\n    residue.\n 3. DO NOT BOUND metrics/blame.py and metrics/git_metrics.py TIGHTLY. git blame and log walks over a\n    long-lived tickets branch are legitimately slow and hold NO store lock; a low bound would produce\n    spurious `unavailable` metrics. A high watchdog (e.g. 300s) is fine.\n 4. ADD JITTER to _acquire_fcntl (50ms), _acquire_mkdir (100ms) and gitutil._with_index_lock_retry\n    (0.2 x attempt). Copy git's shape: multiply each sleep by uniform [0.75, 1.25].\n 5. ADD AN AGE CEILING to _mkdir_lock_is_stale for the refuse-without-proof branches, following\n    git gc's 12-hour precedent. Choose far above any legitimate hold (the write lock is held for a\n    single event append — seconds), e.g. 1 hour. Preserves yaw-gravel-linen conservatism in the\n    normal case while REMOVING THE PERMANENT-WEDGE FAILURE CLASS ENTIRELY.\n    *** SINGLE HIGHEST-VALUE CHANGE, FULLY BACKED BY PRIOR ART. ***\n 6. GIVE THE BOOT PATH GIT'S --auto SEMANTICS. mcp_server.py:705 already passes timeout=5,\n    attempts=1 — good, consistent with gc --auto. But run_ensures logs Lo … [truncated by reconciler]","jira_comment_id":"963788","timestamp":1786690438666627738},{"author":"reconciler","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"OSS RESEARCH, PART 2 — GIT LOCK INTERNALS AND TOOLS THAT DRIVE GIT CONCURRENTLY (2026-07-31)\n\nCompanion to the previous comment. Deeper primary-source read. VERSION ANCHOR: git.git master @ a97fcc37c (2026-07-30); stable v2.55.0 (2026-06-29); prior v2.54.0 (2026-04-19). Two lock-relevant things landed RECENTLY and are easy to miss: core.lockfilePid (in 2.54+) and core.configLockTimeout (merged to master 2026-07-30, NOT YET IN ANY RELEASE).\n\n== 1. GIT ITSELF ==\n\n1.1 THE LOCKFILE MODEL — and the key negative finding. lockfile.h L4-31: \"Mutual exclusion and atomic file updates... we create a lockfile <filename>.lock, write the new file contents into it, and then rename the lockfile to its final destination... with O_CREAT|O_EXCL... Automatic cruft removal. If the program exits after we lock a file but before the changes have been committed, we want to make sure that we remove the lockfile. This is done by remembering the lockfiles we have created in a linked list and setting up an atexit(3) handler and a signal handler that clean up the lockfiles.\" Also: \"lockfiles only block other writers. Readers do not block, but they are guaranteed to see either the old contents of the file or the new contents.\" *** ON \"WHY DOES GIT DELIBERATELY NOT DO STALE-LOCK DETECTION\": THERE IS NO SUCH COMMENT, AND THAT IS ITSELF THE FINDING. *** lockfile.h and tempfile.h contain ZERO occurrences of \"stale\". The lockfile API has no concept of lock ownership, age, or liveness. Staleness is not rejected by an argued design note — it is simply OUTSIDE THE MODEL. A lock is a file that exists; cleanup is atexit+signal handlers in the owning process; anything surviving SIGKILL, OOM, power loss or container teardown is THE HUMAN'S PROBLEM. No mailing-list thread found arguing it either way.\n\n1.2 THE LOCK_* FLAGS (lockfile.h L155-178). There is NO \"LOCK_ON_ERROR\". The three are:\n  LOCK_DIE_ON_ERROR (1) — \"If a lock is already taken for the file, die() with an error message.\"\n  LOCK_NO_DEREF (2) — do not resolve symlinks; \".lock\" appended to the path argument itself.\n  LOCK_REPORT_ON_ERROR (4) — return -1 AND give the usual error message.\n  Default (flags 0) is SILENT -1.\n\n1.3 THE TIMEOUT MACHINERY. lockfile.h L180-190: \"If the file is currently locked, retry with quadratic backoff for at least timeout_ms milliseconds. If timeout_ms is 0, try exactly once; if timeout_ms is -1, retry indefinitely.\" Backoff is quadratic WITH JITTER, capped per-sleep at 1s. From commit 044b6a9ef \"lockfile: allow file locking to be retried with a timeout\" (Michael Haggerty, 2015-05-14): \"On each failed attempt, use select() to wait for a backoff time that increases quadratically (capped at 1 second) and HAS A RANDOM COMPONENT TO PREVENT TWO PROCESSES FROM GETTING SYNCHRONIZED.\" CONFIGURED TIMEOUTS (Documentation/config/core.adoc L580-598):\n  core.filesRefLockTimeout   ms  default 100    0=no retry  -1=forever   shipped 2.15+\n  core.packedRefsTimeout     ms  default 1000   0=no retry  -1=forever   shipped 2.5+\n  core.configLockTimeout     ms  default 1000   0=no retry  -1=forever   MASTER ONLY, UNRELEASED\n  credentialStore.lockTimeoutMS ms default 1000                          shipped 2.30+\nWHY THE TWO REF NUMBERS DIFFER — the load-bearing rationale, and it also explains the index. From f4ab4f3ab \"lock_packed_refs(): allow retries when acquiring the packed-refs lock\" (2015):\n  \"This is not such a limitation for loose reference files. First, they don't take long to rewrite.\n   Second, MOST REFERENCE UPDATES HAVE A KNOWN 'OLD' VALUE, so if another process is updating a\n   reference at the same moment that we are trying to lock it, then probably the expected 'old' value\n   will no longer be valid, and the update will fail anyway. But these arguments do not hold for\n   packed-refs: * The packed-refs file can be large and take significant time to rewrite. * Many\n   references are stored in a single packed-refs file...\"\nFrom 4ff0f01cb \"refs: retry acquiring reference locks for 100ms\" (2017), which walked that back:\n  \"The philosophy of reference locking has been, 'if another process is changing a reference, then\n   whatever I'm trying to do to it will probably fail anyway because my old-SHA-1 value is probably\n   no longer current'. But this argument falls down if the other process has locked the reference to\n   do something that DOESN'T ACTUALLY CHANGE THE VALUE of the reference, such as pack-refs or reflog\n   expire.\"\nHaggerty's operational number (mailing list): before the patch, \"maybe 10-50 such errors every day across our whole infrastructure\"; after, \"that number went basically to zero.\" Jeff King noted GitHub was already running equivalent logic.\n\n1.4 index.lock: CONFIRMED — NO RETRY KNOB EXISTS, ANYWHERE. repository.c L471-478 repo_hold_locked_index() -> repo_hold_lock_file_for_update(), defined in lockfile.h L236-241 as ..._timeout(..., 0) = TRY EXACTLY ONCE. All ~40 call sites (builtin/add.c:454, builtin/commit.c:534, builtin/checkout.c:630, apply.c:4966, add-interactive.c:645, ...) inherit that. git grep over Documentation/, *.c, *.h for indexLockTimeout|index_lock_timeout|INDEX_LOCK_TIMEOUT returns NOTHING. There is no core.indexLockTimeout and never has been. NO patch or RFC proposing one was found; the 100ms ref-lock thread contains no index discussion at all. GIT'S CHOSEN ANSWER INSTEAD — commit 27344d6a6 \"git: add --no-optional-locks option\" (Jeff King, 2017), the closest thing to an official position. It enumerates three options and REJECTS the coordination one:\n  \"1. Have some kind of 'pseudo-lock' that allows other commands to tell status that they want the\n      lock. THIS IS LIKELY TO BE COMPLICATED AND ERROR-PRONE TO IMPLEMENT (and maybe even impossible\n      with just dotlocks to work from, as it requires some inter-process communication).\n   2. Avoid background runs of commands like 'git status'... This is awkward...\n   3. Ask 'status' not to lock or write the index. This is easy to implement.\"\nAnd why index contention differs from ref contention: \"taking the index lock may conflict with other operations in the repository. Especially ones that the user is doing themselves, which _aren't_ opportunistic. In other words, 'git status' knows how to back off when somebody else is holding the lock, but other commands don't know that status would be happy to drop the lock if somebody else wanted it.\" SHIPPED MECHANISM: `git --no-optional-locks <cmd>` / GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 (Documentation/git.adoc):\n  \"If this Boolean environment variable is set to false, Git will complete any requested operation\n   without performing any optional sub-operations that require taking a lock. For example, this will\n   prevent 'git status' from refreshing the index as a side effect. THIS IS USEFUL FOR PROCESSES\n   RUNNING IN THE BACKGROUND WHICH DO NOT WANT TO CAUSE LOCK CONTENTION with other operations on the\n   repository. Defaults to 1.\"\nIt is an ENV VAR deliberately, so it propagates to subprocesses (Peff). *** READING: git's answer to \"many agents driving one repo\" is NOT \"wait for the index lock\" — it is \"DON'T TAKE IT.\" ***\n\n1.5 WHAT GIT TELLS THE USER, AND THE 2026 PID-FILE CHANGE. Through v2.53 (unchanged since aed7480ca, 2016; wording origin e43a6fd3e, 2009 — \"Just saying that index.lock exists doesn't tell the user _what_ to do\"): \"Unable to create '%s.lock': %s.\\n\\nAnother git process seems to be running in this repository, e.g. an editor opened by 'git commit'. Please make sure all processes are terminated then try again. If it still fails, a git process may have crashed in this repository earlier: remove the file manually to continue.\" In v2.54+ replaced by dbdcab6b8 \"lockfile: add PID file for debugging stale locks\" (Paulo Casaretto, 2026-01-22). lockfile.c L254-299 picks one of three:\n  pid_status == 1  -> \"Lock may be held by process %ju; if no git process is running, the lock file\n                       may be stale (PIDs can be reused)\"\n  pid_status == -1 -> \"Lock was held by process %ju, which is no longer running; the lock file\n                       appears to be stale\"\n  else             -> \"Another git process seems to be running in this repository, or the lock file\n                       may be stale\"\nConfig core.lockfilePid (core.adoc L351-360), DEFAULT FALSE: \"Git will create a PID file alongside lock files... named by inserting ~pid before the .lock suffix... contains a single line in the format `pid <value>`.\" The `~` is deliberate (lockfile.h L132-137): \"The tilde is forbidden in refnames and allowed in Windows filenames, avoiding namespace collisions.\" THREE THINGS TO TAKE FROM THIS:\n 1. Even in 2026, with dead-PID detection in hand, GIT STILL DOES NOT BREAK THE LOCK. It prints\n    \"appears to be stale\" and stops. The commit is titled \"for DEBUGGING stale locks.\"\n 2. Git annotates its own PID-liveness check with \"(PIDs can be reused)\" IN USER-FACING TEXT. It does\n    not trust pid liveness as proof.\n 3. The PID file records NO HOSTNAME — unlike gc.pid. On a shared/network filesystem the kill(pid,0)\n    check is therefore meaningless-to-actively-wrong. Off by default, so latent, but a real gap.\n\n1.6 gc.pid — GIT'S OWN HOSTNAME+PID STALENESS SCHEME (the one place it does break a lock). builtin/gc.c L718-781, predicate decoded: gc backs off only if ALL hold — pidfile younger than 12h; parses as \"<pid> <hostname>\"; AND (host differs — in which case we cannot check, so ASSUME ALIVE — OR kill(pid,0) says the local pid is alive, OR errno==EPERM).\n  /* 12 hour limit is very generous as gc should never take that long... running gc --auto one day\n     late is not a big problem. --force can be used in manual gc after the user verifies that no gc\n     is running. */\n  time(NULL) - st.st_mtime <= 12 * 3600 &&\n  fscanf(fp, scan_fmt, &pid, locking_host) == 2 &&\n  /* be gentle to concurrent \"gc\" on remote hosts */\n  (strcmp(locking_host, my_host) || !kill(pid, 0) || errno == EPERM);\nFailure message (gc.c:999): \"gc is already running on machine '%s' pid %ju (use --force if not)\". Origin 64a99eb47 (2013): \"In the worst case, gc may be delayed 12 hours if a daemon reuses the pid stored in gc.pid.\" The design CONSCIOUSLY ACCEPTS A 12-HOUR FALSE-NEGATIVE WINDOW as the price of not racing. THREE STRUCTURAL SAFEGUARDS WORTH NAMING: (a) a WALL-CLOCK CEILING (12h) as backstop for any pid/hostname confusion; (b) an explicit HOSTNAME GUARD THAT FAILS CLOSED — a different hostname means \"assume running\", never \"assume stale\"; (c) errno==EPERM treated as alive (pid exists under another uid). CONTAINER CAVEAT, HONESTLY SCOPED: the mechanism has an obvious container failure mode (hostname is the ephemeral container/pod id, so a repo on a mounted volume gc'd from a NEW container after a hard kill sees strcmp != 0 -> fails closed -> gc refuses FOR UP TO 12 HOURS). The inverse also holds if hostnames are reused. NO git-project bug report, mailing-list thread, or upstream acknowledgement of either case was found. Treat the mechanism analysis as sound (read from source) but the CONTAINER INCIDENCE as UNDOCUMENTED, not a cited known issue. RELATED: `git maintenance` does NOT use this scheme — gc.c L1784-1800 takes a plain $GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY/maintenance.lock with LOCK_NO_DEREF: no pid, no hostname, no timeout, no staleness check. Same for schedule.lock at L3368. THE PID+HOSTNAME LOGIC IS `git gc` ONLY. gc.log: on --auto in daemonized mode stderr is captured into .git/gc.log.lock and committed to .git/gc.log iff non-empty (process_log_file(), gc.c L78-104). gc.logExpiry (config/gc.adoc L70-75): \"If the file gc.log exists, then 'git gc --auto' will print its content and exit with status zero instead of running unless that file is more than 'gc.logExpiry' old. Default is 1.day.\"\n\n1.7 DOES GIT EVER AUTO-REMOVE STALE LOCK FILES? Only its own, only in-process. atexit(3) + signal handlers (tempfile.c) roll back locks the CURRENT process holds, including on die(). Nothing scans for or reaps foreign locks. SIGKILL, OOM, power loss or container SIGKILL leaves the .lock in place PERMANENTLY. The only exceptions in the whole tree are gc's guarded gc.pid takeover and the gc.log unlink.\n\n== 2. TOOLS THAT DRIVE GIT CONCURRENTLY ==\n\n2.1 git-lfs — NO index.lock HANDLING AT ALL (a negative example). @b9964490 (2026-07-28). grep -rn \"index\\.lock\" over the tree: ZERO matches. grep -rni \"another git process\": zero. It never opens .git/index; it shells out to `git update-index -q --refresh --stdin` (git/git.go:645 UpdateIndexFromStdin), streamed through gitIndexer (commands/pull.go L185-234). Its ONLY concurrency guard is an in-process sync.Mutex serialising its own writes to that one subprocess's stdin. On failure it logs and continues (pull.go:153) — that string is what users see when the child dies on index.lock (#2937, #937, #2817). It has historically also CREATED the problem — #558 \"Failures during a checkout can leave the git index locked\": killing update-index mid-stream orphans the lock. A PR search for index.lock in git-lfs returns 0 results; NO ONE HAS EVER ADDED HANDLING. git-lfs does NOT use GIT_INDEX_FILE — the only occurrence is canonicalizeEnvironment() (commands/commands.go:391), which merely normalises an INHERITED value. So `git lfs pull`/`checkout` contend for the real index. The locking/ package is unrelated (server-side LFS file locks over HTTP/SSH). No OS advisory locks anywhere: grep -rniE \"flock|LOCK_EX\" finds nothing. The one retry loop is Windows sharing-violation handling (robustRemoveRetryDelaysMs = [0,1,10,20,40]).\n\n2.2 git-annex — THE STRONGEST DESIGN IN THE ECOSYSTEM. (DataLad mirror, upstream/master @ e3a0558c.) FIRST, THE STRUCTURAL MOVE: git-annex NEVER CONTENDS FOR .git/index.lock. It commits to the git-annex branch through a private index via GIT_INDEX_FILE — Git/Index.hs:17 (indexEnv = \"GIT_INDEX_FILE\"), Annex/GitOverlay.hs L26-63 (withIndexFile -> .git/annex/index), used by Annex/Branch.hs:688. SECOND, WHEN IT DOES BREAK A LOCK IT DOES SO UNDER A PROVEN INVARIANT, NOT A TIMER — Annex/Branch.hs L668-681 prepareModifyIndex: \"Since all modifications of the index are performed inside this module, and only when the journal is locked, the fact that the journal has to be locked when this is called ensures that no other process is currently modifying the index. So any index.lock file must be stale, caused by git running when the system crashed, or the repository's disk was removed, etc.\" => THE GENERAL PATTERN: A HIGHER-LEVEL LOCK ESTABLISHES THE INVARIANT THAT MAKES BREAKING THE LOWER-LEVEL LOCK SAFE. TWO BACKENDS. Utility/LockFile.hs disclaims portability: \"Posix and Windows lock files are extremely different. This module does *not* attempt to be a portability shim.\" Utility/LockFile/Posix.hs uses real fcntl record locks with two details worth stealing: tryLock runs under uninterruptibleMask_ so async exceptions cannot orphan a held fd; and checkSaneLock compares the fd's deviceID/fileID against a fresh stat of the path — \"This check is useful if the lock file might get deleted by something else\" (the classic fcntl unlink footgun). WHY PID LOCKS EXIST (man page): \"Normally, git-annex uses fine-grained lock files to allow multiple processes to run concurrently... That works great, unless you are using git-annex on a filesystem that does not support POSIX fcntl locks. This is sometimes the case when using NFS or Lustre... And, some networked filesystems are so inconsistent that one node can't reliably tell when the other node is holding a pid lock. CAVEAT EMPTOR.\" Defaults (Types/GitConfig.hs L255-257): annex.pidlock = False; annex.pidlocktimeout = 300 seconds. `git annex init` auto-enables by PROBING — taking a real exclusive lock in a temp dir and flipping the config on failure (Annex/Init.hs L458-464), with a 10s watchdog warning because the probe itself hangs on sick NFS. THE SIDE LOCK — THE GENUINELY NOVEL BIT. Joey Hess (devblog day 336): \"the fun part about pid locks is that it can be pretty hard to tell if one is stale or not. Especially when using a networked filesystem, because then the pid in question can be running on a different computer. Even if you do figure out that a pid lock is stale, how do you then take over a stale pid lock, without racing with another process that also wants to take it over?... PUT A MORE MODERN LOCK FILE SOMEPLACE ELSE (eg, /dev/shm) AND USE THAT LOCK FILE TO LOCK THE PID LOCK FILE. Then you can tell if a local pid lock file is stale quickly locally, and take it over safely.\" Mechanism (Utility/LockFile/PidLock.hs):\n - Pid-lock CONTENT is PidLock { lockingPid, lockingHost } — hostname recorded exactly because the pid\n   may be on another machine.\n - Side lock is a real POSIX exclusive lock in /dev/shm (fallback /tmp), i.e. ON LOCAL STORAGE THE\n   NETWORK FS CANNOT LIE ABOUT; name = abs pid-lock path with / -> _, md5-prefixed if truncated.\n   Deliberately world-writable: \"Since /dev/shm and /tmp are sticky dirs, a user cannot delete another\n   user's lock file there, so could not delete a stale lock.\"\n - RELEASE ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING: dropSideLock UNLINKS BEFORE UNLOCKING — \"Delete the file first,\n   to ensure that any process that is trying to take the side lock will only succeed once the file is\n   deleted, and so will be able to immediately see that it's taken a stale lock\" — and dropLock drops\n   the side lock BEFORE the pid lock, \"at which point the pid lock will be considered stale.\"\n - TAKEOVER REQUIRES BOTH CONDITIONS: \"Since we have the sidelock, and are on the same host that the\n   pidlock was taken on, we know that the pidlock is stale, and can take it over.\" DIFFERENT HOSTNAME\n   => REFUSE, fall through to timeout. Residual limit stated plainly in the haddock: \"if the lock file\n   is on a networked file system, and was created on a different host than the current host\n   (determined by hostname), this can't be done and stale locks may persist.\"\n - Acquisition is link()-based per open(2)'s advice, verifying linkCount == 2 plus full stat equality,\n   with O_EXCL fallback where hardlinks are unsupported — plus an explicit LUSTRE INSANITY CHECK\n   (\"link has been observed to incorrectly *succeed*, despite the dest already existing\"), detected by\n   scanning the directory for two entries with the same name (devblog day 337).\n - CRITICALLY: linkToLock Nothing _ _ = return Nothing — WITHOUT THE SIDE LOCK IT WILL NOT EVEN\n   ATTEMPT ACQUISITION. The side lock is a PRECONDITION, not just a diagnostic.\nRETRY POLICY: FLAT 1-SECOND POLL, NO EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF, HARD ABORT. waitLock: \"Uses a 1 second wait-loop, retrying until a timeout. After the first second waiting, runs the callback to display a message, so the user knows why it's stalled\" -> \"waiting for pid lock file ... which is held by another process (or may be stale)\", then at exhaustion \"Gave up waiting for pid lock file ...\". TWO-TIER LOCKING (Utility/LockPool/PidLock.hs L34-43): \"There are two levels of locks. A STM lock is used to handle fine-grained locking among threads... The pid lock handles locking between processes.\" SELF-DEADLOCK ACROSS SUBPROCESSES is real and handled out-of-band: one pid lock per repo means a process holding it that spawns git, which invokes `git-annex filter-process`, deadlocks against itself. Fix is an env var PIDLOCK_<mangled-abs-path>=1 signalling \"your parent holds it\" (PidLock.hs L322-336), applied AD HOC — commit 0485dd316 explains why not globally: \"it would need to take the pid lock when running any and all processes, and that would be a problem when git-annex runs two processes concurrently.\"\n\n2.3 libgit2, dulwich, JGit/Gerrit, gitoxide. LIBGIT2 (@ddf3b5c8): NO retry, NO timeout, NO config support. src/util/filebuf.c L43-51 is one existence check + one O_CREAT|O_EXCL (futils.c L69-88), EEXIST -> GIT_ELOCKED. No loop, no sleep. grep for filesRefLockTimeout|packedRefsTimeout -> ZERO HITS; libgit2 does NOT honour git's timeout configs. Index message (index.c L3858-3866): \"the index is locked; this might be due to a concurrent or crashed process\". GIT_ELOCKED = -14. The de-facto contract is in libgit2's OWN TESTS — tests/libgit2/threads/refdb.c L45-93 wraps every call in `do { ... } while (error == GIT_ELOCKED);` — AN UNBOUNDED BUSY-SPIN WITH NO SLEEP, a model to note, not to copy. Maintainer position on stale locks (libgit2#809): cleanup deferred to the APPLICATION, with user approval. Live NFS contention bug: libgit2#6858 (open since Aug 2024, no maintainer response). Windows-only retry (do_with_retries, posix_w32.c L160-197) is for AV/sharing violations, not .lock contention. DULWICH (@d1c5d905): same, simpler. dulwich/file.py L210-234 — one os.open(O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_EXCL), FileExistsError -> FileLocked. No retry, no sleep, and GitFile() HAS NO TIMEOUT PARAMETER TO PASS. Zero hits for the git timeout config names. FileLocked is raised and NEVER CAUGHT ANYWHERE in dulwich. The close() docstring names the hazard: \"If this method fails, it will attempt to delete the lockfile. However, it is not guaranteed to do so... which will prevent future writes to this file until the lockfile is removed manually.\" JGIT (@3f3beede) — PARTIAL. LockFile.createLockFileWithRetry() is MISLEADINGLY NAMED: one retry, only for NoSuchFileException (racing mkdirs), NOT for contention. The only contention backoff is packed-refs-only and HARDCODED — RefDirectory.java L123-125: RETRY_SLEEP_MS = (0,100,200,400,800,1600) (~3.1s, six attempts, then LockFailedException); the setter is package-private, NOT OPERATOR-CONFIGURABLE. Loose ref locks get nothing — RefUpdate returns Result.LOCK_FAILURE immediately. Zero hits for filesRefLockTimeout/packedRefsTimeout. JGit DOES add an in-process fair queue, and the comment is the best statement of the two-tier idea found (RefDirectory.java L157-171): \"This lock exists so multiple threads in the same process can wait in a fair queue without trying, failing, and retrying to acquire the on-disk lock.\" GERRIT — RETRIES THE OPERATION, NOT THE LOCK. THE MOST TRANSFERABLE PATTERN. ExceptionHookImpl.java L34-58: shouldRetry(...) { return isLockFailure(throwable); }. Consumed by RetryHelper with WaitStrategies.join(exponentialWait(maxWait=5s), randomWait(50ms)) under a 20s total budget, documented as retry.maxWait / retry.timeout in config-gerrit.txt: \"Maximum time to wait between attempts to retry an operation when one attempt fails (e.g. on NoteDb updates due to contention, aka lock failure, on the underlying ref storage). Operations are retried with exponential backoff, plus some random jitter... Defaults to 5 seconds.\" Gerrit preserves the causal chain so the retry can fire — MergeOp.java:1076: \"Lock failures are a special case: RetryHelper depends on this specific causal chain in order to trigger a retry.\" RETRYING THE WHOLE LOGICAL OP IS WHAT MAKES IT SAFE: it re-reads base state. GITOXIDE (@b95db7ca) is THE ONLY NON-C IMPLEMENTATION AT PARITY. gix-lock/src/acquire.rs L13-20:\n  pub enum Fail { #[default] Immediately, AfterDurationWithBackoff(Duration) }\n  /// Retry after failure with quadratically longer sleep times to block the current thread.\nquadratic-with-jitter backoff, error PermanentlyLocked { resource_path, mode, attempts } hinting \"The lockfile ... might need manual deletion\"; and it HONOURS BOTH GIT KEYS WITH GIT'S DEFAULTS (gix/src/config/cache/access.rs L220-238). SCOREBOARD: gitoxide = PARITY; JGit = PARTIAL + HARDCODED; libgit2 & dulwich = NOTHING.\n\n2.4 AVOIDANCE PATTERNS — DON'T TAKE THE LOCK. GIT_INDEX_FILE PER OPERATION. git(1): \"This environment variable specifies an alternate index file.\" Git's own canonical user is `git stash` — builtin/stash.c L502-521 states the reason verbatim:\n  /* We need to run restore files from a given index, but without affecting the current index, so we\n     use GIT_INDEX_FILE with run_command to fork processes that will not interfere. */\nplus in-process equivalents write_index_as_tree(..., stash_index_path.buf, ...), create_index_from_tree(), set_alternate_index_output(). `git filter-branch` does it globally (git-filter-branch.sh:286: GIT_INDEX_FILE=\"$(pwd)/../index\"; export GIT_INDEX_FILE) and its docs teach users the trick for --index-filter. git-annex uses it for its whole branch. libgit2's no-env equivalent is git_index_new() — \"Create an in-memory index object.\" *** WARNING: A PRIVATE INDEX REMOVES index.lock CONTENTION BUT NOT refs/**.lock CONTENTION — the final update-ref still hits the shared ref store. *** PURE PLUMBING, NO INDEX AT ALL: hash-object -w -> mktree (or scratch-index update-index --index-info + write-tree) -> commit-tree -> update-ref; only the last step locks, and only one ref. The cleanest in-tree proof is `git notes` — notes-utils.c L23-65 does write_notes_tree() -> commit_tree() -> refs_update_ref(), CREATING NO index.lock EVER. Application exemplars: git-appraise (Google) stores review data in git-notes under refs/notes/devtools, \"Each item stored is written as a single line of JSON\" — line-per-item PRECISELY SO CONCURRENT WRITES MERGE rather than conflict; GitHub's Git Data API is this model over HTTP (blobs->trees->commits->refs, base_tree for incremental builds, no checkout); josh rewrites whole histories via libgit2 treebuilders, with its single repo.index() call confined to the explicit \"snapshot my dirty worktree\" input mode. git worktree PARTITIONS THE INDEX, NOT THE REFS. git-worktree docs: each linked worktree gets its own \"files such as HEAD, index, etc.\", but \"In general, all pseudo refs are per-worktree and all refs starting with refs/ are shared... SINCE REFS ARE SHARED ACROSS ALL WORKTREES, except refs/bisect, refs/worktree and refs/rewritten.\" And \"By default, the repository config file is shared across all worktrees\" — so config.lock is shared t … [truncated by reconciler]","jira_comment_id":"963789","timestamp":1786690438681463109}],"created_at":1785542629558834001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"287a254f-f265-4a8d-89aa-8043f0df89b0","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"c2ba-98bf-ca2d-481a"},{"link_uuid":"c0e5d7c4-15fd-4bf3-b75d-1568303bfeee","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"983f-ee85-1e06-428e"},{"link_uuid":"939a22a4-a847-4fc0-a037-e8e0468376dd","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"e43f-0d14-13f9-408f"},{"link_uuid":"3feeaad3-201b-4450-b1b8-dfb0467567ab","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"e161-596c-0b7e-42af"},{"link_uuid":"8139cd23-91f1-49ff-b7a6-328bdd07fa42","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"f95f-a049-04b1-4219"},{"link_uuid":"2a42a21c-dd0b-4d5c-92b1-c9d697a17d8a","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"428c-b750-3507-49e6"},{"link_uuid":"46e49bc4-7f5c-4750-9c30-f089ca59b699","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"d98d-55de-435d-4285"}],"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nrebar is driven by MANY local agent processes at once — high local concurrency is the normal\noperating mode, not an edge case. Concurrent rebar sessions contend on LOCAL git operations\nagainst the shared tickets store (`.git/index.lock`, ref locks such as `HEAD.lock` /\n`packed-refs.lock`), and today that contention surfaces as errors to the agent running the\ncommand instead of being absorbed. Prior tickets bounded the NETWORK-capable call sites\n(747f, 77e1, 983f) and fixed the store-lock identity/wedge class (304e, 5389); this ticket\ncovers the remaining class: local git call sites with no contention policy and (for several)\nno bound at all — `_commands/compact.py`, `_commands/delete.py`, `_commands/fsck.py`,\n`_commands/txn.py`, `_store/event_append.py`, `metrics/blame.py`, `metrics/git_metrics.py`.\n\nThe OSS research this ticket commissioned is recorded in the comments above and informs the\ndesign (git lockfile.c's jittered backoff, Gitaly's lock knobs + watchdog deadlines, JGit's\nown-lock-before-file-lock serialization, Mercurial's bounded wait, Bazel's \"print the holder,\nnever parse it\").\n\nOPERATOR-RATIFIED DECISION (2026-08-04, recorded as a comment above): handle local git lock\ncontention AUTOMATICALLY, with NO escalation to the agent — a per-store advisory lock plus\nbounded jittered retry/backoff on the store's git call sites (consistent with the existing\npush-retry pattern in `rebar._store.push`); transient conflicts resolve silently (at most a\ndebug/warning log); only a genuinely stuck lock (bounded budget exhausted) surfaces one\nactionable error naming the lock file with holder guidance. Defaults on the order of tens of\nseconds, jittered; NO new config keys (no store/git config surface exists today).\n\n## Approach\n\nAll changes at the store's local git execution seam so every routed call site inherits them:\n\n1. **Per-store advisory git-op lock** (`rebar._store.gitutil`): taken inside\n   `_with_index_lock_retry` — the shared seam EVERY index-mutating store git invocation\n   already routes through (`run_git_write` for txn/compact/delete, AND\n   `event_append`'s `_git_add`/`_git_commit`/`_git_rm`/`_git_commit_paths` composition used\n   by `stage_and_commit`/`batch_stage_and_commit` and the UU/invalid-object recovery) — so\n   the append/batch path is covered without touching its call sites. It serializes those\n   invocations across rebar processes with a kernel `fcntl.flock` on a per-store lock file\n   inside the tracker's git dir. Fast path is a single\n   non-blocking `flock` (zero added latency, no sleep, when uncontended). When contended, poll\n   with short jittered sleeps up to a bounded budget, logging at debug. The kernel drops the\n   lock when its holder dies, so there is NO staleness/reclamation logic to get wrong (the\n   Bazel/cargo property cited in the research). On budget exhaustion, log a warning and\n   proceed WITHOUT the advisory lock — it is a contention reducer; correctness stays with the\n   git-level retry below (JGit's in-process fair lock pattern).\n2. **Bounded jittered retry on git's own lock signatures** (`_with_index_lock_retry`):\n   extend the retried signature from index.lock-only to git's ref/config lock conflicts\n   (\"cannot lock ref\", \"<name>.lock': File exists\"); jitter every backoff sleep by\n   uniform[0.75, 1.25] (git lockfile.c's exact shape, research §1a); raise the retry budget\n   from ~2s to the order of tens of seconds (default ~20s summed backoff). Stale-index-lock\n   reclamation semantics are unchanged and still apply only to index.lock.\n3. **One actionable exhaustion error**: when the budget is exhausted and the failure is still\n   a lock signature, the returned `CompletedProcess.stderr` is augmented with the lock file\n   path (parsed from git's own message when present) and holder guidance (\"held by another\n   git process; if none is running, remove the lock file\"). txn's and delete's `_git`\n   wrappers already raise `CommandError` from `cp.stderr`, so they surface it with no\n   per-site change; compact.py's failure branches (compact.py lines ~378 and ~386) today\n   DISCARD `cp.stderr` and print a fixed generic message, so those two branches are changed\n   to include the git stderr in the written error — that is the one per-site change needed\n   for the exhaustion message to reach the operator everywhere.\n4. **Jitter the store write-lock contention sleeps** (`rebar._store.lock`): `_acquire_fcntl`\n   (fixed 50ms) and `_acquire_mkdir` (fixed 100ms) get the same uniform[0.75, 1.25] jitter —\n   research rec #4 (thundering-herd de-synchronization). Budgets/semantics unchanged.\n5. **Watchdog bounds for the remaining local-only sites** (AC 2 of the original filing):\n   `run_git_write` gets a generous local watchdog (120s, folded into a synthetic rc-124\n   result exactly like `event_append._run_git`), covering txn/compact/delete. `fsck.py` has\n   TWO read-only git call sites and both are bounded at 120s: the `_git` closure inside\n   `_tracker_sync_status` (via `run_git`, rc-124 fold) and `_branch_mismatch`'s direct\n   `subprocess.run` (a `timeout=120` whose `TimeoutExpired` is caught and yields the\n   existing best-effort `None`). `metrics/blame.py` gets `timeout=300` — its existing broad\n   `except Exception -> None` already absorbs `TimeoutExpired`. `metrics/git_metrics.py`\n   gets `timeout=300` with `TimeoutExpired` left to PROPAGATE (no rc-124 fold — its `_git`\n   is `check=True`/returns stdout): `_commands/metrics.py` wraps each `evaluate` call in\n   per-metric fault isolation (metrics.py line ~81) that renders the metric unavailable, the\n   correct degraded state (research 5b rec 3: blame/log walks are legitimately slow, hold no\n   store lock — watchdog-grade only). `event_append` keeps its existing 30s.\n   These are watchdogs against a wedged filesystem/lock, NOT latency budgets — deliberately\n   NOT copied from the 300s/30s network values but justified against local latency\n   (research §2: \"watchdog-grade bound is defensible; latency-grade is not\").\n\nBlocking policy under local contention, stated explicitly (AC 4 of the original filing):\n**bounded jittered retry** — serialize rebar's own writers via the advisory lock, ride out\ntransient git lock conflicts silently with jittered backoff to a tens-of-seconds budget, and\nfail with ONE actionable error only when the budget is exhausted (stuck lock). Chosen over\nblock-forever (cargo/Bazel) because rebar's callers are unattended agents with their own\ndeadlines, and over fail-fast because the observed conflicts resolve on retry.\n\nNo new config keys: constants are module-level (the established `_INDEX_LOCK_*` pattern) so\ntests can tune them; no config surface for store/git behavior exists to extend.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py` — advisory lock, jitter helper, ref-lock signature, raised\n  jittered budget, actionable exhaustion stderr, 120s watchdog in `run_git_write`\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — jittered contention sleeps (two `time.sleep` call sites)\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` — bound BOTH read-only git call sites at 120s (the\n  `_tracker_sync_status` closure `_git` and `_branch_mismatch`'s direct `subprocess.run`)\n- `src/rebar/_commands/compact.py` — include git stderr in the two failure branches that\n  today discard it (so the exhaustion guidance reaches the operator)\n- `src/rebar/metrics/blame.py`, `src/rebar/metrics/git_metrics.py` — 300s watchdog\n- `tests/interfaces/store/test_git_contention_9305.py` (new) — the RED-first behavioral suite\n- existing tests under `tests/interfaces/store/` + `tests/unit/` adjusted only if the raised\n  budget/jitter breaks a timing assumption\n\n## Testing\n\nSTRICT TDD (operator condition): RED behavioral tests first, real git repos in tmp dirs with\na competing lock holder — behavioral/contractual, not change-detectors:\n\n- (a) an index-mutating store git op hitting a transient `index.lock` held briefly by a\n  competing process succeeds after retry with NO error surfaced (result rc 0);\n- (b) a permanently held lock produces ONE actionable error after the bounded budget — the\n  final failure names the lock file and includes holder/remedy guidance;\n- (c) uncontended ops add no latency: `time.sleep` instrumented to record calls — zero sleeps\n  on the happy path — and the advisory-lock fast path is non-blocking;\n- plus: a ref-lock signature (\"cannot lock ref\") is retried and self-heals; two concurrent\n  processes appending through the seam both succeed (advisory serialization).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The OSS research findings are recorded on this ticket and the chosen approach cites\n      them rather than asserting a value (checked: research comments above; Approach cites\n      §1a/§2/§4/5b per item).\n- [x] A transient index.lock or ref-lock conflict on a store git write self-heals with no\n      error surfaced to the caller (behavioral test (a) + ref-lock test pass against a real\n      competing lock holder).\n- [x] A permanently held lock yields exactly one actionable error naming the contended lock\n      file and the remedy, after a bounded jittered budget on the order of tens of seconds\n      by default (behavioral test (b) passes; message includes the lock path and guidance).\n- [x] Uncontended store git writes add no latency: no sleep on the happy path (behavioral\n      test (c) passes with an instrumented `time.sleep`).\n- [x] Each local-only git call site listed in Context is either explicitly bounded with a\n      watchdog justified against LOCAL latency (not copied from the network value) or\n      documented as deliberately unbounded with the reason stated (checked by reading every\n      touched wrapper, including BOTH fsck sites; `fsck.py` no longer conflicts with 01e8,\n      which is closed).\n- [x] rebar's blocking policy under local contention is stated explicitly in\n      `gitutil.py`'s module docs (bounded jittered retry + advisory serialization; silent\n      transient recovery; one actionable error on a stuck lock), justified against the\n      research, and applied consistently at the seam.\n- [x] make lint, make typecheck, and the targeted test files exit zero.","env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","file_impact":[{"path":"src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py","reason":"advisory lock, jittered bounded retry, actionable exhaustion error, watchdog"},{"path":"src/rebar/_store/lock.py","reason":"jitter the contention sleeps"},{"path":"src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py","reason":"bound the local _git wrapper (120s watchdog)"},{"path":"src/rebar/metrics/blame.py","reason":"300s watchdog on git subprocess"},{"path":"src/rebar/metrics/git_metrics.py","reason":"300s watchdog on git subprocess"},{"path":"tests/interfaces/store/test_git_contention_9305.py","reason":"new RED-first behavioral contention suite"}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","managed_refs":[["relates_to","428c-b750-3507-49e6"],["relates_to","983f-ee85-1e06-428e"],["relates_to","c2ba-98bf-ca2d-481a"],["relates_to","d98d-55de-435d-4285"],["relates_to","e161-596c-0b7e-42af"],["relates_to","e43f-0d14-13f9-408f"],["relates_to","f95f-a049-04b1-4219"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"45e34ed5-abcf-490a-86f6-ce248dd3a747","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"closed","tags":[],"ticket_id":"9305-b42c-3262-4c58","ticket_type":"bug","title":"Local-only git call sites are unbounded under high concurrent local use; contention policy is unspecified","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":[]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787175154217087001,"uuid":"d00a8ad1-c43f-4138-89c8-cef99fe9d09f"}